#block(inset:12pt,fill:gray,radius:3pt)[
*Abstract.* This method note describes a proposed simulation architecture for incompressible liquids, viscous liquids, granular media, snow, elastic solids with cutting, and low-Mach reacting gases. All continua share *one* staggered marker-and-cell (MAC) velocity representation with a candidate sparse adaptive hierarchy: every velocity lives on faces, every scalar at cell centres, and there are no nodal velocities. Particles carry transport and constitutive history; the grid evaluates stress divergence, contact, viscosity, and a variational pressure projection. We state the fourteen-step sub-step, the affine transfer (APIC storing $C$, per-component deposition and gather moments, full-stencil ghost gathering, ASFLIP), the integrated finite-volume pressure equation with moving walls, ghost-fluid free surfaces and two-phase series coefficients, the viscous and capillary operators, the narrow band with an Eulerian interior, particle split and merge, the reduced low-Mach gas model, the tracked surface and whitewater stages, and the persistent state, memory, transaction, determinism and checkpoint contracts. This is a design synthesis: no production solver or CUDA benchmark has been executed, and no simulation validation result is claimed.
]

= Scope, terminology, and conventions <sec-scope>
A *continuum* selects a set of governing equations and a particle layout: `Liquid`, `Granular`, `Snow`, `Elastic`, or `Gas`. A *substance* supplies parameter values (water, oil, honey, dry sand, powder snow, rubber, smoke). A *phase* identifies an immiscible region (water and oil are two phases of `Liquid`), whereas a *body* is a connected component for contact: a solid object, one side of a cut, or a Blender object. Two substances may mix within one phase.

The first end-to-end deliverable is a breaking wave with spray, foam and bubbles: an 8 × 20 × 8 m domain, 3.5 cm cells near the camera, 240 frames in at most three hours including all stages and I/O on a 16 GB RTX 5060 Ti with adaptivity (at most six hours tolerated temporarily without it). The reference scene is fixed: 3 m depth at the inlet, then a sloping beach to 0 m over 20 m; second-order Stokes swell with $H approx 1$ m and $T approx 4$ s generated by the Ocean Boundary (the paddle is a test variant); a fixed camera 2 m from the breaking point, 35 mm, 1920 px; 24 fps. The host is a Ryzen 7 9700X (8 cores), 64 GB DDR5 and 3 TB of disk; Mesh uses about six CPU cores in parallel with Simulate. Viscous and multi-fluid flow, sand and snow, elastic solids with cutting, then low-Mach gas follow in that delivery order. The architecture is sized for all of them from the start; implementation follows the order.

*Sign conventions* (used everywhere): $phi<0$ inside the liquid, or inside phase $a$; $n=nabla phi\/|nabla phi|$ is the outward normal; $kappa=nabla dot n$, so $kappa=2\/R>0$ for a spherical drop; $hat(e)_"up"=-g\/|g|$. Let $x$ denote position, $t$ time, $u$ Eulerian velocity, $rho$ density, and $b$ body acceleration. Continuum balances are
$ D rho \/ D t + rho nabla dot u=0, quad rho D u \/ D t=nabla dot sigma_C+rho b, quad D\/D t=partial_t+u dot nabla. $
$sigma_C$ denotes Cauchy stress, plain $sigma$ the surface-tension coefficient of a phase pair, $gamma$ the heat-capacity ratio of a gas, and $dot(gamma)$ a shear rate. At an interface between phases $a$ and $b$, $n$ points from $a$ to $b$ and jumps are $[q]=q_b-q_a$. $Delta x_ell=Delta x_0 2^ell$ is the cell size at level $ell$. Indices: $p$ particles, $i, j$ cells or faces, $a$ velocity components, $f$ faces, $c$ children or source cells. $V_0$ is a reference volume, $V$ a current volume, $a_f$ a physical open face area (m²), and $A_f in [0,1]$ a dimensionless obstacle aperture.

For liquids $sigma_C=-p I+2mu D(u)$. For solids $sigma_C$ follows an elastic energy and plastic history. For low-Mach gas the thermodynamic pressure $p_0$ is uniform per pressure component and the projected divergence need not vanish. Whitewater is a one-way visual dispersed phase.

This paper distinguishes owner requirements, provisional numerical choices, acceptance obligations and measured evidence (@sec-open). Equations below fix sign, volume and unit conventions; changing them requires changing implementation and validation together.

= One staggered grid for every continuum
== Transport, storage, and spatial unknowns
The material point method (MPM) is a hybrid Lagrangian–Eulerian discretisation. Material points move with the body and carry mass, reference volume, velocity and deformation history; a grid receives their contributions, evaluates forces and contact, and returns velocity. Scratch transfer accumulators reset each sub-step; particle history and persistent Eulerian velocity, occupancy, pressure and gas inventories retain their declared ownership [1, 3]. A MAC grid is a staggered placement of unknowns: pressure at cell centres and each normal velocity component at the centre of the faces perpendicular to its axis, which pairs face pressure gradients with cell flux divergence and avoids the collocated checkerboard.

== One velocity representation
FluidiTy places *all* velocities of *all* continua on the faces of a single MAC grid, one component $a$ per face normal to $a$. Pressure $p$, signed distance $phi$, phase fractions and gas scalars live at cell centres. There is no nodal velocity anywhere. Solids use MPM on a staggered grid (Stomakhin et al. 2014 [18]); affine transfers use MAC-APIC (Ding, Shinar & Schroeder 2020 [2]). Each component has its own quadratic B-spline support on its own shifted lattice.

#figure(align(center,grid-figure()),caption:[Left: one staggered grid. Cells store $p$, $phi$ and fractions; faces store one normal velocity component; particles of every continuum deposit into the face lattices. Right: in 1D, a coarse quadratic B-spline whose degrees of freedom sit at cell centres is an exact combination of four fine ones (mask $(1,3,3,1)\/4$), and a coarse cubic B-spline centred on a face is an exact combination of five fine ones (mask $(1,4,6,4,1)\/8$). Quadratic splines centred on corners or faces do not nest under dyadic refinement.])

The owner requirement is a single MAC velocity representation (D3). The following arguments motivate its candidate adaptive realisation. *(i) Nesting.* Truncated hierarchical B-splines (THB) at 2:1 transitions in this candidate require the coarse basis to be spanned by fine functions. A quadratic B-spline is nested by dyadic refinement only when its degrees of freedom sit at cell centres. On MAC faces, the transverse components are cell-centred along their transverse axes and stay quadratic; the normal component is face-centred along its own axis and becomes a *cubic* B-spline in the M3 candidate, which nests. Then $D_(p a)="diag"(Delta x^2\/3$ on $a$, $Delta x^2\/4$ elsewhere$)$, against $Delta x^2\/4 I$ for the uniform quadratic M1–M2 kernel. A bounded THB experiment must confirm the actual nesting and patch identities after the uniform baseline; storage is identical in both cases. *(ii) No dual representation.* Maintaining collocated solid velocities beside MAC fluid velocities would require a second representation and a conservative face-to-node coupling; the single-grid architecture avoids that obligation. *(iii) Native boundary treatment.* Ghost-fluid free surfaces, obstacle apertures and integrated fluxes are face quantities. Voronoi or power cells (no tensor-product B-splines, high cost) and a Rhie–Chow-stabilised collocated grid (checkerboard modes, no native ghost fluid) are rejected.

The simulation interface (`sdf_sim`, fractions, θ, κ) is owned by Simulate. A rendered surface is reconstructed afterwards by a separate stage at a different resolution; output smoothing is never a physical capillary model, and Simulate never reads it.

== Candidate sparse hierarchy and size control <sec-blocks>
In the candidate layout a *block* holds $4^3$ cells and owns its 64 cells and the $3 times 64$ low-side faces; high-side faces are read from neighbours through the block table. For component $a$ and axis $d$, samples sit at $(k+s_(a d))Delta x$ with $s_(a d)=0$ if $d=a$ and $1\/2$ otherwise. Quadratic support starts at $b_d=floor(x_d\/Delta x-s_(a d)-0.5)$; cubic normal support starts at $b_d=floor(x_d\/Delta x)-1$. Particle bins use the common half-offset rule $B_d=floor((x_d\/Delta x-1.5)\/4)$, independent of component. A bin owns a shared $8^3$ tile with indices $4B_d,...,4B_d+7$. For displacement at most $0.5Delta x$ this contains every uniform quadratic and cubic shifted support. THB requires checking its actual expanded support separately. Every contribution checks tile inclusion before writing; an overflow uses the global path and requests RETRY_DT if a required block is absent. The target displacement CFL is $0.45Delta x$, based on the actual advecting velocity. An open-addressed GPU hash table maps 64-bit keys (4-bit level and three signed 20-bit offset coordinates, with reserved empty and tombstone values) to dense block indices. Neighbouring blocks differ by at most one level. A 4:1 ratio is rejected: it would split a particle into 64, make one coarse face face 16 fine faces, and THB is only guaranteed under gentle grading. The cited sources do not validate this 3D GPU THB-MPM MAC combination; this is the first project risk, with a go/no-go after an independent uniform reference and real-GPU baseline. Adaptivity is a firm objective (D9): if THB nesting or the patch test fails, an adaptive alternative on boxes (GIMP-style transitions as in AG-MPM, Gao et al. 2017) is tried first, again with a patch test; meanwhile M3 delivers "uniform sparse + narrow band" (≈ 6.8 GB) with FC1 relaxed to at most six hours, and adaptivity stays an open milestone rather than being abandoned.

The requested cell size is an upper bound $h_"req"=min(h_"phys",h_"interface",h_"support",h_"camera",h_"max")$ with $h_"camera"<=sqrt(8 "err"\/|kappa|)$ and $"err"="pixel size" dot "depth"\/"focal length"$. Refinement is immediate when $Delta x>h_"req"$. Eight sibling blocks coarsen only if $2Delta x<=0.8 h_"req"$ on the parent and its halo (support plus CFL displacement) *uninterrupted* for a hysteresis time $T_"hyst"$ of accepted physical time; any veto resets the counter. Vetoes are unresolved interfaces or cavities, sheets or gaps thinner than $4Delta x$, CPIC bands (the finest level is forced within $2Delta x_"coarse"$ of blades), body contact, and singular moments. The camera may request detail, never remove it below the physical minimum. A refused coarsening does not reject the step.

= The sub-step
@tab:substep lists the fourteen steps of the common numerical contract in order; Figures 11a–11c expand them. The ordering fixes the time stage of every transfer, force, boundary condition and state update.

#figure(table(columns:(.35fr,1fr,3.2fr),inset:4pt,stroke:.3pt,align:left,
 [*Step*],[*Name*],[*Content and ordering contract*],
 [①],[Emit],[Particle and fuel sources (initial volume, inflow) with frozen $Delta t$; IDs from a scan on (parent id, ordinal).],
 [②],[Sort],[Stable counting sort per block; fused into the previous G2P2G only when @sec-gpu allows it.],
 [③],[P2G],[After activation and reset, per component $a$, over structurally allocated compatible pairs $cal(C)_p$ with renormalised $N'$, centred $r'$ and $D'$: stress ($tau=0$ for Liquid and Gas), mass, pure momentum and force in separate i64 accumulators. Mass validity is decided in ④.],
 [④],[Snapshot],[Face validity from deposited mass; Eulerian fallback and overlap blending; $u^0=q/m$ where valid; save $v_"old"=u^0$ before any force.],
 [⑤],[Forces and thermo],[Apply stress, gravity and external fields after the snapshot; Gas receives residual buoyancy and conservative thermodynamic updates, without a second full gravity.],
 [⑥],[Waves],[Ocean Boundary relaxation and absorbing beach on $u$ (after the snapshot).],
 [⑦],[Contact],[MPM bodies: per-body face velocities and Coulomb friction. Liquid and Gas: no-penetration is imposed by the projection.],
 [⑧],[Interface],[Geometry at $x^n$: `sdf_sim`, fractions, apertures, θ, κ, $beta_f$, $J_f$, $s_"corr"$.],
 [⑨],[Viscosity],[One variational grid operator over band and interior; explicit only with a certified spectral bound, otherwise implicit; fractions from ⑧; before projection.],
 [⑩],[Project],[Compatibility and gauge only for noncompliant Neumann components; solve and correct with identical $k_f$, $J_f$. Last physical write to $u$ before ghost construction and G2P.],
 [⑪],[G2P],[Frozen complete basis $(x^n,N^n,nabla N^n)$ with old/new ghost samples: $delta$ before overwrite; affine fit $C$ using $D^"full"$; true gradient $L$; deformation and ReturnMap; $J$, $beta$, $v$; contact on stored and advecting velocities; continuous position sweep.],
 [⑫],[Accumulate],[Whitewater birth events per source cell and accepted sub-step, owned by the trial.],
 [⑬],[Validate],[Finite, admissible, true pressure residual and divergence relation → ACCEPTED, else RETRY_DT, CAPACITY or FATAL.],
 [⑭],[Adapt],[Scheduled refine, coarsen, split, merge and band reseed after acceptance, in a separate transaction.],
),caption:[The sub-step (English design specification). $Delta t$ is chosen by the CFL rules of @sec-dt, reduced on the GPU.])<tab:substep>

Taking the snapshot in ④ before gravity, stress, buoyancy, wave relaxation and contact fixes the force increment independently of its splitting. With consistent particle/old-grid history, the unconstrained free-fall oracle requires acceleration $g$ within 0.1 % at every energy setting. Interface geometry in ⑧ is evaluated at $x^n$ from the simulation's own `sdf_sim`; the implicit viscosity in ⑨ needs its fractions and precedes the projection, so the velocity read by G2P is the projected one.

Figures 11a–11c at the end of this note expand @tab:substep. Arrows cannot express every dependency; the contracts that matter most are: the FLIP snapshot precedes all forces (④); interface geometry precedes viscosity, which precedes the projection (⑧, ⑨, ⑩); the projection is the last write to $u$; inside G2P, $delta$ is computed before any overwrite, ReturnMap precedes $beta_p$, particle contact acts on $v_p$ before $x_p$ moves, and $alpha$ appears once in the position update; force and deformation pair outside cut bands, with their discrepancy and boundary work recorded inside them; whitewater events belong to the trial; acceptance advances the clock once; adaptation follows acceptance.

= Affine particle–grid transfer on the MAC grid
== Stored state and moments
A PIC particle stores $v_p$ only. APIC stores the affine model $v_p+C_p(x-x_p)$; $C$ has units s⁻¹ and is an affine fit, generally distinct from the true gradient $L$. Storing $C$ rather than the support-dependent moment $B=C D$ avoids an artificial scale change when support changes, but does not preserve affine energy or angular momentum automatically (@sec-remap). Let $r_(i p a)=x_(i a)-x_p$, with $x_(i a)$ the basis's Greville abscissa. Deposition uses the compatible centred moment $D'_(p a)$ of @sec-masks; gathering uses
$ D^"full"_(p a)=sum_(i in cal(S)_(p a)) N_(i p a) r_(i p a) r_(i p a)^T. $
$cal(S)_(p a)$ is the complete original support, including ghost samples. Without a cut, uniform quadratics give $D^"full"=Delta x^2/4 I$; transitions require the actual full matrix. If either required moment is ill-conditioned or supporting mass insufficient, local PIC is counted and `kAffineOff` is set. The slot `C` then carries the diagnostic true gradient $L$; every affine consumer accesses zero through a typed accessor. Split and merge refuse flagged particles.

#figure(align(center,stencil-figure()),caption:[Affine reconstruction on the shifted face lattices. Deposition uses compatible samples; gathering completes the original basis with ghosts and uses its own moment.])

== P2G, grid update, G2P
For the compatible deposition support, with $r'=r-mu$ and weights of @sec-masks,
$ m_(i a)=sum_p N'_(i p a)m_p, quad q_(i a)=sum_p N'_(i p a)m_p(v_(p a)+c_(p a) dot r'_(i p a)), quad f_(i a)=-sum_p V_(0p)(tau_p nabla N'_(i a)(x_p))_a. $
$ v_("old",i a)=q_(i a)/m_(i a), quad u_(i a)=v_("old",i a)+Delta t(f_(i a)/m_(i a)+g_(K,a)+f_("fields",a)). $
Here $g_K=0$ for Gas, whose buoyancy is the residual relative to ambient hydrostatic pressure. Activation and reset precede deposition; structural allocation does not depend on the yet unknown mass. Mass, momentum and force are quantised separately before any sum (@sec-gpu). The old-grid snapshot is taken after band blending and before every force.

For gathering define complete sample arrays $hat(u)^n$ and $hat(u)^(n+1)$ on the frozen original support. Compatible valid samples read the pre-force snapshot and projected final velocity respectively. Missing or incompatible samples are extrapolated separately from these two time stages on the particle's own side; prescribed blade-normal values use the blade velocity at the matching time stage. The old array must never be reconstructed from the post-force field. Affine reproduction requires affine-complete extrapolation of a field compatible with the boundary. Arbitrary extrapolation followed by normal replacement does not establish it. Then
$ v_("pic",a)=sum_i N_(i p a)hat(u)^(n+1)_(i a), quad delta_a=v^n_(p a)-sum_i N_(i p a)hat(u)^n_(i a), $
$ c_(p a)=sum_i N_(i p a)hat(u)^(n+1)_(i a)r_(i p a)^T (D^"full"_(p a))^(-1), quad L_p=sum_a e_a sum_i hat(u)^(n+1)_(i a)nabla N_(i a)(x_p)^T, $
$ v_p=v_"pic"+alpha delta, quad w_p=v_"pic"+beta_p alpha delta, quad x_p <- x_p+Delta t w_p. $<eq:asflip>
If the support touches an Eulerian fallback face, set $delta=0$ explicitly. Within ⑪, compute $delta$ before overwriting the particle velocity; gather $C$ and $L$ separately; form $F_"trial"=(I+Delta t L)F$; reject nonpositive determinant; apply ReturnMap; evolve Liquid and Gas $J <- J exp(Delta t tr L)$; choose $beta$ from the post-return state; then constrain both $v_p$ and $w_p$ by contact and continuously sweep the trajectory (@sec-contact). $alpha$ occurs once in each update [4].

The conservation properties derived for MAC-APIC [2] depend on its support and position convention. They do not extend automatically to ghost replacement, pressure projection, adaptive resampling or ASFLIP. APIC dissipates some modes. No-force FLIP tests must use both old and new ghost arrays through this executable sequence.

== Energy slider and separation
The artist sees one control, *energy* $e in [0,1]$ (default 0.6), with $alpha=0.99 e$ and $beta_max=e$ (design choice). The separation coefficient is per particle, $beta_p in {0,beta_max}$, following Fei et al. [4, §5.3] (the exact criterion is to be re-checked against the source). Liquid particles separate when $tr L_p>0$ and $phi>-2Delta x$ (free-surface band), and never near an obstacle. Granular and Snow particles separate when $det F_E>=1$ after the plastic return and out of contact. The initial Gas baseline uses APIC with $alpha=beta=0$, without liquid free-surface separation; later gas blending has a separate transport/effect gate. *Elastic particles always have $beta_p=0$*: a non-zero $beta$ displaces an elastic body without generating stress. The dam-break look-book must show a useful, measured change with $e$ while retaining force-response and stability gates; no universal monotonic-energy theorem is assumed.

*Bounded M1 regular-cell transport.* In a stationary aligned tank, use a six-face field only for a whole containing pressure cell of source-free incompressible liquid with zero surface tension and compatible usable projected faces. An aligned, fully sealed stationary face supplies its prescribed zero normal speed. For local coordinate $xi_a$ and signed Cartesian face speeds $u_(a,"lo")$ and $u_(a,"hi")$,
$ w_a = u_(a,"lo") + xi_a (u_(a,"hi") - u_(a,"lo")), quad
  D_h = sum_a (u_(a,"hi") - u_(a,"lo")) / Delta x, quad J_p <- J_p exp(Delta t D_h). $
The lowest-order Raviart–Thomas field has divergence $D_h$, equal to the uncut finite-volume divergence, and a shared constant normal trace. Its transverse velocities can jump across faces. The stored PIC/history velocity and affine fit are unchanged; this path bypasses separation for positions while retaining contact and the displacement check. The original transport covers cut or unsupported cells, moving walls, source or prescribed divergence, compliance, and capillarity. The exponential material volume update is distinct from the determinant of the discrete position map. Adaptive interfaces need separate qualification.

In a translating tank, a particle in a selected pressure cell at step start draws direct new-history carrier values and continuation donors only from usable faces incident to the selected pressure cells. Other particles use the complete carrier history. The old gather history and published carrier keep their original eligibility.

#figure(align(center,transfer-figure()),caption:[Compatible centred deposition and complete old/new ghost gathering have distinct weights and moments. Exact stress–deformation work pairing applies outside cut bands; cut replacement work and reactions are recorded.])

== Renormalised masks for cutting and inactive faces <sec-masks>
CPIC [3] excludes particle–face pairs across a blade. Structurally unavailable faces are excluded from deposition as well; mass validity is determined afterwards. Per component, over the compatible set $cal(C)_p$,
$ S_p=sum_(i in cal(C)_p)N_(i p), quad N'_(i p)=N_(i p)/S_p, quad nabla N'_(i p)=(nabla N_(i p)-N'_(i p)sum_(j in cal(C)_p)nabla N_(j p))/S_p, $
$ mu_p=sum_i N'_(i p)r_(i p), quad r'_(i p)=r_(i p)-mu_p, quad D'_p=sum_i N'_(i p)r'_(i p)r'_(i p)^T. $
Thus $sum N'=1$ and $sum nabla N'=0$: centred deposition preserves particle momentum and gives zero net internal force for constant stress. Gathering instead uses the complete $N,nabla N,r,D^"full"$ above. If $S_p<0.1$ or $D'$ is singular, use counted free flight/local PIC, suppress separation and affine deposition, and constrain both velocities before advection. Skipping the gather must not skip the position update.

= Stress, contact, and constitutive models
== Stress measures and the work identity
Let $F=partial x/partial X$, $J=det F$, and $V=J V_0$. For energy $Psi$ per reference volume, $P=partial Psi/partial F$ and $tau=P F^T=J sigma_C$. Solids return $tau$ for force deposition $f_(i a)=-V_(0p)(tau_p nabla N'_(i a))_a$; an extra $J$ would count volume change twice. Liquid and Gas return zero particle stress: viscosity is a grid operator.

#figure(align(center,stress-figure()),caption:[Stress measures and volume conventions. Kirchhoff stress rescales Cauchy stress by $J$; deposition and deformation pair on complete unmasked supports.])

Outside cuts, force and deformation use the same true basis gradient, giving $sum_i u_i dot f_i=-sum_p V_(0p)tau_p:L_p$. The MLS replacement $N D^(-1)r$ is not used for deformation or stress force. On a cut support define $L'_p$ from compatible $nabla N'$ and real grid samples; the force work is $-sum V_0 tau:L'$, while deformation uses the complete ghost $L$. Record the work discrepancy $Delta W_"cut"=Delta t sum V_0 tau:(L-L')$, the impulse and torque introduced by boundary replacement, and blade work. Count each velocity-replacement reaction once; never convert an arbitrary work residual into an impulse. Equal and opposite boundary impulses and torques enter the owning body; a prescribed blade's external actuator ledger receives its reaction. This records the cut approximation rather than asserting exact internal work pairing there. Ordinary $C$ is never substituted for $L$.

== Granular media: Hencky elasticity and Drucker–Prager return
State: $x, v, C, m, V_0, F_E, log J_"pl", "body"$. With $F_E=U Sigma V^T$ and $epsilon=log Sigma$, $tau=U(2mu epsilon+lambda tr(epsilon) I)U^T$. The return of Klár et al. [7] acts on the trial singular values of $F_"trial"=(I+Delta t L)F_E$:
$ hat(epsilon)=epsilon-(tr epsilon\/3)I, quad alpha_"DP"=sqrt(2\/3) (2 sin phi_f)\/(3-sin phi_f), quad delta gamma=norm(hat(epsilon))+(3lambda+2mu)\/(2mu) tr(epsilon) alpha_"DP". $
Use the translated-cone coordinate $t=tr epsilon-c_"coh"$. The apex branch $t>=0$ precedes any norm division: set $epsilon=(c_"coh"/3)I$ (zero for cohesionless sand). Otherwise use $delta gamma=norm(hat(epsilon))+(3lambda+2mu)t alpha_"DP"/(2mu)$. If $delta gamma<=0$ the trial is elastic; otherwise $epsilon <- epsilon-delta gamma hat(epsilon)/norm(hat(epsilon))$. The apex branch handles zero deviatoric norm without division. Reconstruct $F_E=U exp(epsilon)V^T$ and update $log J_"pl"$ by the trial-minus-return log determinant. Cohesion here is a dimensionless translated logarithmic-strain cone parameter, requiring material calibration. Artist controls are friction angle and cohesion; tests include the pile angle, zero tensile stress for cohesionless material, and the cohesive isotropic apex.

== Snow
Following Stomakhin et al. [19], $F_E=R_E S_E$, $J_E=det F_E$, $J_"pl"=exp(log J_"pl")$ and
$ tau=2mu(F_E-R_E)F_E^T+lambda(J_E-1)J_E I, quad mu=mu_0 e^(xi(1-J_"pl")), quad lambda=lambda_0 e^(xi(1-J_"pl")). $
The trial $F_"trial"=U Sigma V^T$ is clamped to $Sigma_c="clamp"(Sigma,1-theta_c,1+theta_s)$, $F_E=U Sigma_c V^T$, and $J_"pl" <- J_"pl" det Sigma\/det Sigma_c$, which keeps $det F_E J_"pl"=J_"total"$ (`snow_jp_invariant`). One artist control, compactness, drives $xi$, $theta_c$, $theta_s$ and $E_0$.

== Elastic solids and cutting
Compressible neo-Hookean (default) and fixed-corotated (option, stable under large rotations):
$ tau=mu(F F^T-I)+lambda ln J I, quad tau=2mu(F-R)F^T+lambda(J-1)J I, $
with $mu=E\/(2(1+nu))$ and $lambda=E nu\/((1+nu)(1-2nu))$; here $mu$ is a shear modulus, not a viscosity. Elastic models require $J>0$. Controls are stiffness $E$ and bounce (Rayleigh damping on $L$); $beta_p=0$. For cutting, each blade $k$ defines an unsigned distance $d_k$ and band $B_k={d_k<r_"cut"}$. A face carries $"affinity"_k=[i in B_k]$ and $"side"_k$; a particle memorises $"side"_k(p)$ when it enters $B_k$ (persistent, after BladeMaster). A pair is compatible iff for all $k$, $"affinity"_k(i) and "affinity"_k(p) => "side"_k(i)="side"_k(p)$. Incompatible pairs leave $cal(C)_p$; in G2P the blade-normal component takes the blade velocity. The two sides of a cut become two bodies. Damage fracture (CD-MPM) is deferred.

== Contact, boundaries, and rigid bodies <sec-contact>
Blender obstacles become solver-side SDFs with sampled velocity. Fluids see them *only* through apertures and $u_"obst"$ in the projection (@sec-pressure); MPM bodies see them through a face contact rule: with $v_"rel"=v-v_"obst"$ and $v_n=v_"rel" dot n<0$, retain the incoming $v_n$ for the friction limit, set the outgoing normal component to zero, and use $v_t <- max(0,|v_t|-mu|v_n|)hat(t)$, with the tangential part reconstructed from the four neighbouring faces. Store the incoming normal velocity before zeroing the outgoing part; Coulomb friction uses that incoming impulse. One artist control, adhesion $mu$, selects sticky, slipping or separable behaviour. In G2P, near-contact inward velocity is removed from both stored $v_p$ and actual advecting $w_p$; active contact sets $beta_p=0$. Sweep the relative trajectory against the time-dependent obstacle to its first contact, apply the rule there, then advance the remaining time subject to renewed contact. Use conservative advancement with certified SDF distance/Lipschitz and motion bounds, or continuous swept-triangle intersection for the represented animated geometry. A fixed grid-relative sample spacing cannot guarantee detection of a thin wall. Final projection out of an obstacle repairs numerical penetration but is not collision detection. Velocity changes contribute $m_p Delta v$, matching torque and moving-wall work to the owning obstacle or actuator ledger. This also constrains inward velocity from the FLIP residual.

Faces touched by several simulated bodies (water on sand, water against rubber) carry one velocity per body ($K<=4$, design choice) in a sparse table, merged or separated by Coulomb with normals from mass gradients (Nairn; DC-APIC; IQ-MPM). Exceeding $K$ raises E-CONTACT-BODY-CAPACITY rather than gluing bodies together.

*Fluid against simulated MPM bodies* (design choice, partitioned). In the projection, the face fraction occupied by an MPM body is treated as an obstacle: $A_f=1-"solid fraction"_f$ and $u_("obst",f)$ is the body face velocity after ⑦. The resulting pressure acts on the body at the next sub-step, $f_(i a) += p_f (|f|-a_f) n_f$ distributed over the body faces, with $n_f$ outward from the fluid. Overlapping blockers require their geometric union and unambiguous wall motion, or a counted veto; summing overlapping solid fractions does not give exact union geometry. The one-sub-step lag is a known added-mass risk for light bodies; it is monitored by a floating-elastic-cube test and a logged added-mass ratio, with a monolithic coupling (IQ-MPM) as fallback.

A wave paddle is an animated obstacle: it pushes water through the moving-wall flux of the projection. The Ocean Boundary applies, in ⑥ after the snapshot, $u <- u+(1-e^(-Delta t\/tau(xi)))(u_"target"-u)$ with $tau(xi)=tau_0\/w(xi)$, $w(xi)=(e^(xi^3.5)-1)\/(e-1)$ (Jacobsen et al. [20]); the water level is driven toward $eta_"target"$ by emitting or deleting particles, and second-order Stokes waves subtract the return current. An absorbing beach uses the same form with a rest target.

*Two-way rigid bodies (decided, M6).* In v1 a Blender object is a one-way obstacle; two-way coupling arrives with solids at M6. With $n_f$ outward from the body, $F_B=-sum_f p_f (|f|-a_f)n_f+sum "contact impulses"\/Delta t+m_B g$ plus the matching torque, integrated by Newton–Euler, and the body is reinjected as an obstacle with $u_"obst"=v_B+omega_B times (x-x_B)$. The coupling is partitioned and explicit; added-mass ratios are monitored, with sub-iterations if needed. The acceptance test is a 500 kg/m³ cube floating at half height within ±2 %.

The rigid-body table stores positive mass, body-frame inertia, centre, unit quaternion, linear/angular velocity and force/torque accumulators; pose/history is checkpointed. With $I_"world"=R I_"body"R^T$, integrate
$ dot(v)=F/m, quad dot(omega)=I_"world"^(-1)(T-omega times(I_"world"omega)), $
and orientation consistently with world angular velocity while preserving quaternion normalisation. Pressure and contact reactions enter the torque once. Rotation/sweep bounds use this same pose evolution; translation alone is not a complete rigid-body update.

== Time step <sec-dt>
The advective target is $Delta t<=0.45Delta x_min/v_max$, with $v_max$ bounding the actual particle $w_p$, Eulerian band faces, source velocities and obstacle motion. Sources and walls are evaluated before emission without consuming IDs; the particle estimate comes from the previous step and is checked a posteriori. The shared-tile inclusion limit is $0.5Delta x$ for uniform kernels; actual THB supports are checked individually. To reach a frame boundary, choose $n=ceil((t_"frame"-t)/Delta t_max)$ and $Delta t=(t_"frame"-t)/n$, avoiding tiny residual steps.

Solids require $Delta t<=C_s Delta x/c_K$, where `K::WaveSpeed` certifies a bound on the largest eigenvalue of the full acoustic tensor over propagation directions in the current returned state, using the reference density and current hardened tangent. Principal-axis Hencky coefficients alone do not bound oblique modes. Directional checks require a certified enclosure of the unsampled directions; an unqualified sampled maximum is insufficient. Loss of ellipticity or a nonpositive acoustic mode is an admissibility failure with an explicit diagnostic, not a negative square root. For a principal frame with positive singular values $s_i$, let $A_(i j k l)=partial^2 Psi/(partial F_(i j)partial F_(k l))$ and $B_(i j k l)=A_(i j k l)s_j s_l$. Major symmetry makes the flattened $B$ symmetric; the acoustic tensor is its restriction to $a ⊗ n$, an isometry for unit $n$. Thus the conservative bound
$ c_K^2=(1/rho_0)max_(i j)sum_(k l)|B_(i j k l)|, quad rho_0=m/V_0, $
controls every direction. It is a safe upper bound, not an exact wave speed. For Hencky $t_i=2mu ln s_i+lambda ln J$, the nonzero blocks are $A_(i i i i)=(2mu+lambda-t_i)/s_i^2$, $A_(i i j j)=lambda/(s_i s_j)$ and, for $i!=j$,
$ a_(i j)=(t_i-t_j)/(s_i^2-s_j^2), quad b_(i j)=((s_j/s_i)t_i-(s_i/s_j)t_j)/(s_i^2-s_j^2), $
with shear block $mat(a_(i j),b_(i j);b_(i j),a_(i j))$. At equal stretches use limits $a=mu/s_i^2$, $b=(mu-t_i)/s_i^2$. The row-sum bound follows from these three normal and three shear blocks; use stable divided differences near equality. Other laws use their full consistent tangent or a proved sharper bound. Reject nonfinite or unsupported states, including singular values below the declared floor; never hide loss of ellipticity by clipping a negative squared speed. $C_s$ still accounts for the discrete spatial stencil. Independent directional differentiation checks validate the implementation of the enclosure.

For the corotational and snow energy $Psi=mu sum_i(s_i-1)^2+lambda(J-1)^2/2$, the normal tangent block is $A_(i i i i)=2mu+lambda(J/s_i)^2$, $A_(i i j j)=lambda(2J-1)J/(s_i s_j)$. Its shear coefficients are $a_(i j)=2mu(1-1/(s_i+s_j))$, $b_(i j)=2mu/(s_i+s_j)-lambda(J-1)J/(s_i s_j)$. Hardened moduli are frozen during this elastic linearization; plastic updating is separate. These analytic blocks feed the same spatial-tangent row bound, avoiding a dense Hessian per particle.

Explicit viscosity requires $Delta t Lambda_mu<=2(1-epsilon_"stab")$ with a certified assembled spectral upper bound (@sec-viscous-bound); otherwise use the implicit branch. Add the capillary bound of @sec-tension and the cavity bound $Delta t<=0.5/omega_"cav"$ of @sec-weak. GPU reduction selects the bound without a host read. Per-region stepping is deferred.

= Pressure projection <sec-pressure>
== Integrated finite-volume form <sec-pfv>
The projection follows Batty et al. 2007 and Ng et al. 2009 [21, 22]. For each face $f$ oriented $i -> j$, with $|f|$ the geometric face area,
$ a_f=A_f |f|, quad d_f="centre distance" (theta d_f "if cut by the free surface"), quad k_f=Delta t a_f beta_f\/d_f, quad beta_f=1\/rho_f. $
The row of cell $i$ is
$ sum_(j "unknown") k_f (p_i-p_j)+sum_(f "Dir") k_f p_i = b_i, $<eq:pressure>
$ b_i = & V_i s_i-sum_f a_f u^star_f n_(i f) \
        & -sum_f (|f|-a_f)u_("obst",f) n_(i f) \
        & -sum_(j "unknown") k_f J_f+sum_(f "Dir") k_f p_Gamma. $
and the correction uses the same $k_f$, $beta_f$, $J_f$:
$ u_f=u^star_f-(Delta t beta_f\/d_f)(p_j-p_i-J_f). $<eq:correct>
$V_i$ is the fluid volume of the cell and $u_"obst"$ the velocity of the obstacle (animated, deformable, paddle) on the blocked part of the face. Without the moving-wall term the paddle does not push water; `piston_work` requires the displaced volume to match $integral u_"obst" dot n dif A dif t$ within 1 %. Units: $k_f p$ and every right-hand term are volume fluxes in m³/s. Rows are *not* normalised by $V_i$.

*Uniform translating piston.* On a selected low-x wall link replace the generic blocked-face flux with piston speed times the certified wet area at the frozen start pose. Sum the flux from the immediately higher patch into the column's highest selected head cell only when each potentially wet source crosses the shared edge. The same signed wall flux enters pressure and its defect evaluation. Independent receiving-plane transport still supplies the displaced volume for the piston acceptance test.

*Free surface* (ghost fluid, Gibou et al. [23]): $theta=phi_i\/(phi_i-phi_j)$, bounded below by 0.05 as a documented regularisation; the face becomes Dirichlet with $p_Gamma=p_"gas,gauge"+sigma kappa$ (ambient gas gauge pressure zero). On this Dirichlet link set $J_f=0$: capillarity is already in $p_Gamma$ and must not be counted twice. *Same phase*: $beta_f=1\/rho$ (miscible: $rho$ averaged between $i$ and $j$). *Two immiscible phases* (Kang, Fedkiw & Liu [24]): $theta$ is the fraction of the segment $i -> j$ on the side of $i$'s phase, and continuity of $beta partial p\/partial n$ puts the two half-links *in series*:
$ beta_f=1\/(theta rho_i+(1-theta)rho_j) quad ("no bound on" theta). $
This replaces an arithmetic mean of $1\/rho$, which overestimates the water–air coefficient roughly 250-fold. The oriented capillary jump is
$ [p]=[2mu n dot D n]-sigma kappa, quad J_f=p_("side" j)-p_("side" i)=cases([p] & "if" i in a, -[p] & "if" i in b,) $
with $n$ from $a$ to $b$ and $[q]=q_b-q_a$; in v1 the viscous term is omitted (@sec-visc-int), so $J_f=-sigma kappa_f$ for $i in a$. $J_f$ enters the right-hand side as $-k_f J_f$ in row $i$ and $+k_f J_f$ in row $j$, and @eq:correct with the same sign. For a static drop of $a$, $p_a-p_b=sigma kappa=2sigma\/R$ (`static_drop_jump_orientation`). At M4 the normal viscous stress jump is included.

#figure(align(center,interface-figure()),caption:[Left: an interface crossing a pressure link at fraction θ; the two half-links are resistances in series. Right: at a 2:1 T-junction the coarse face receives exactly the sum of the fine physical fluxes.])

*The pressure metric.* Orient $R_(i f)=+1$ at a link's tail and $R_(j f)=-1$ at its head. Then $D=R "diag"(a)$ maps face velocity to integrated outward fluid flux. The integrated pressure-gradient operator is $G_"grad"=-D^T$; the physical pressure force is $-G_"grad"p=D^T p$. With face inertia
$ M_(f f)=a_f d_f/beta_f>0, quad u^+=u^star-Delta t M^(-1)G_"grad"p, $
the correction agrees with @eq:correct, including its separate boundary/jump lifting. Closed faces are removed or prescribed. This selects one positive discrete quadrature for pressure and viscous kinetic energy; it is not a claim that arbitrary particle-lumped face masses equal geometric dual masses. The velocity-gradient operator is $M^(-1)G_"grad"$, with its mass metric explicit. On a tiny aperture, use the same $a,d,beta$ in mass, conductance and correction rather than silently replacing the inertia in one branch.

*T-junctions and matrix structure.* A coarse face receives the sum of its fine physical fluxes. With $k_f=Delta t a_f beta_f/d_f$, Dirichlet anchoring $R_"Dir"$ and compliance $C$, the integrated pressure matrix is
$ A=R "diag"(k)R^T+R_"Dir"+C=Delta t D M^(-1)D^T+R_"Dir"+C. $
Positive conductance and anchoring give a positive semidefinite matrix, and positive definiteness requires each constant component to be anchored or gauge-restricted. This is an algebraic property of the assembled operators; correct geometry and adaptive consistency require separate tests. Second-order transition accuracy remains a target [6]. The reserved compliance is $C_(i i)=V_i/(rho_i c_i^2 Delta t)$, with $C p^star$ on the right; both vanish in incompressible mode.

== Sources and compatibility
$s_i$ (s⁻¹) separates prescribed physical sources from a numerical Liquid density correction. Define a same-phase cell-centred sensor using declared nonnegative quadrature weights $N_("cell",i p)$,
$ overline(J)_i=(sum_p N_("cell",i p)J_p V_(0p))/(sum_p N_("cell",i p)V_(0p)), quad e_i=-ln(overline(J)_i)/t_"corr", quad t_"corr">0. $
Guard its denominator before evaluation. These are cell weights, distinct from the face-component transfer stencils. In incompressible mode the sensor-supported band uses $s_("corr",i)="clip"(e_i,-s_max,s_max)$ and the particle-free core uses zero. Compressed particles have $overline(J)<1$, giving positive expansion. The time and rate scales are physical parameters validated across $h$ and $Delta t$.

For a sealed zero-compliance component, project only the numerical band correction to bounded zero mean: choose a scalar $lambda$ satisfying
$ sum_(i in "band")V_i "clip"(e_i-lambda,-s_max,s_max)=0, quad s_("corr",i)="clip"(e_i-lambda,-s_max,s_max). $
The monotone scalar solve retains the clamp and leaves core cells at zero; it does not spread a band mean into the core. Physical sources remain separately subject to compatibility. For every component without Dirichlet or positive compliance,
$ sum_i V_i s_i=sum_"walls" (|f|-a_f)u_"obst" dot n. $
Gas evolves its thermodynamic $p_0$ as described in @sec-gas. Any incompatible physical remainder raises E-PRESSURE-INCOMPATIBLE before PCG without a time-step retry. Fix one gauge per unanchored noncompliant component. Positive compliance instead preserves physical mean pressure and receives neither a gauge nor a zero-mean correction.

== Interface geometry and solver
Step ⑧ reduces cut cells to fractions: for each interface cell and phase, a volume fraction, apertures, θ, κ, $beta_f$ and $J_f$, from one plane per cell (value and gradient of `sdf_sim`), stored in FP16 for interface cells only. No polygon is built. A single plane cannot encode both sides of a sheet thinner than a cell or a triple junction; those cases are handled by refinement or by the surface stage (@sec-surface). The solver is PCG preconditioned by matrix-free multigrid on blocks [6, 25], with FP32 vectors and products and FP64 dot products and coarse level. Convergence requires $norm(r)<=epsilon_"abs"+epsilon_"rel" norm(b)$ *and* $max_i |r_i|\/V_i<=epsilon_"div"$ in s⁻¹; with identical assembly and correction the error in the selected incompressible or compliant divergence relation equals $-V^(-1) r$, so an integrated tolerance alone could hide large divergence in tiny cut cells. The true residual is recomputed after the loop. Non-convergence gives RETRY_DT (at most twice) then E-PRESSURE-NOT-CONVERGED. Polyhedral integration of solid stresses is deferred.

= Viscosity
Dynamic viscosity $mu$ is in Pa·s and $nu=mu\/rho$ in m²/s. The operator is always the tensor form $nabla dot (2mu D(u))$ with $D=(nabla u+nabla u^T)\/2$, never $mu Delta u$: the reduction needs constant $mu$ and zero divergence, and fails at free surfaces and between viscosities.

== One full-grid variational operator <sec-viscous-bound>
Step ⑨ applies viscosity over both the particle band and Eulerian interior, using fractions from ⑧. With the same positive face inertia $M_(f f)=a_f d_f/beta_f$ as the pressure metric and symmetric-gradient operator $E$,
$ K_mu=E^T W_mu E, quad W_mu=2mu V, quad b_"wall"=E^T W_mu d_"obs", quad u^T K_mu u>=0, $
where strain is $E u-d_"obs"$, the mass and strain quadratures use the same declared geometry, zero-volume degrees of freedom are removed or prescribed, and off-diagonal tensor terms are counted twice, coefficients at centres and edges are volume-averaged, and the free boundary is traction-free [8]. No particle viscosity is deposited, and $C$ is not used as a stress gradient. M1 uses zero viscosity; the full operator is delivered at M4.

For forward Euler $u^+=u^star+Delta t M^(-1)(b_"wall"-K_mu u^star)$, positive semidefinite $K_mu$ alone does not guarantee dissipation. With stationary homogeneous walls,
$ Delta E=-Delta t u^T K_mu u+(Delta t^2/2)u^T K_mu M^(-1)K_mu u. $
Define $H=M^(-1/2)K_mu M^(-1/2)$ on positive-mass unconstrained degrees of freedom. A conservative Gershgorin bound is $Lambda_mu=max_i(H_(i i)+sum_(j!=i)|H_(i j)|)$; safe assembly or a proved row-sum enclosure includes every contribution and rounds upward with a positive safety margin. Select one branch over each whole coupled component, with the explicit branch allowed only if $Delta t Lambda_mu<=2(1-epsilon_"stab")$ over all its rows; do not mix independent rowwise material decisions. The diffusion-number shortcut $nu Delta t/Delta x^2<=1/12$ is allowed only for a separately certified uniform constant-coefficient stencil. Cuts, slivers, coefficient contrast and level transitions require the assembled bound or implicit fallback.

The implicit branch solves $(M+Delta t K_mu)u=M u^star+Delta t b_"wall"$. Both branches account for prescribed wall work; kinetic-energy decay is asserted relative to that work, not absolutely for moving walls. The subsequent projection enforces the selected incompressible or compliant divergence relation. Herschel–Bulkley materials use the same operator with $mu_"eff"=(tau_y+K dot(gamma)^n)/dot(gamma)$ and $dot(gamma)=sqrt(2D:D)>=dot(gamma)_min$.

== Interfaces between viscosities <sec-visc-int>
In v1, viscosity then pressure is a fractional step. It leaves a normal traction error $2[mu]D_(n n)$ at interfaces between two viscosities, which is accepted and measured (`planar_extension_traction`). Decided (D6): M4 keeps the fractional step first, and moves to a coupled Stokes solve (Larionov et al. [9]) when the predeclared traction or visible-shape gate fails. Before running, freeze analytic or independently converged coupled-Stokes references for planar extension and Couette at viscosity ratios 1, 10 and 1000. Measure area-weighted RMS and maximum normal/tangential traction errors using a nonzero fixture stress scale (for example $2mu_"ref"U/L$) and an absolute floor. The initial gate is 5 % RMS plus a fixture-declared maximum bound at two resolutions and reduced $Delta t$; thresholds cannot be tuned after seeing results. That saddle-point system is indefinite and needs a Schur complement or block preconditioner rather than plain PCG.

= Surface tension <sec-tension>
In v1 capillarity is the jump $sigma kappa$ inside the projection, with $kappa=nabla dot (nabla phi\/|nabla phi|)$ computed on `sdf_sim` *at every sub-step*, at the same time as θ. The tracked mesh is never read by Simulate (`curvature_no_mesh_dependency`). Applying both an area force and the full Laplace jump would double-count capillarity; pressure gradients and capillary jumps must balance in the same discrete operator so that a static drop tests parasitic currents, not only the mean Laplace pressure. $kappa$ is sensitive to second-derivative errors and to interpolation across refinement transitions; filtering $phi$ smooths $kappa$ but shifts the interface and erases thin sheets.

The bounded analytic-sphere field supplies its exact outward curvature $2/R$ on open air links at positive surface tension. The pressure solver seeds the common exterior pressure only when the assembled problem verifies uniform Dirichlet anchors, zero pressure history, zero velocity and prescribed wall flux, zero internal jumps and divergence, and incompressibility. The seed uses the same operator and convergence checks as the ordinary warm start.

The explicit bound (Brackbill) is evaluated per level on active interface cells in the GPU reduction:
$ Delta t<=sqrt((rho_a+rho_b)Delta x^3\/(4pi sigma)). $
For water and air ($sigma=0.0728$ N/m) it is about 0.22 s at $Delta x=3.5$ cm, harmless for the reference wave, but about 1.0 ms at 1 mm, so it controls refined droplets. It scales as $Delta x^(3\/2)$. A semi-implicit version (Zheng 2015, Hyde 2020 [10]) is planned for M4; it changes the operator and its geometric linearisation, not only the time-step check.

The effect test is the $l=2$ mode of a 3D drop of radius $R$ in an outer fluid (Lamb):
$ omega_l^2=l(l-1)(l+1)(l+2)sigma\/(R^3 [(l+1)rho_"in"+l rho_"out"]), $
which reduces to $l(l-1)(l+2)sigma\/(rho R^3)$ for negligible outer density, required within ±5 % at stated resolution, damping and amplitude; plus the static jump $2sigma\/R$ and parasitic currents that decrease with $Delta x$. Contact lines need a wall wetting condition; friction alone does not set an equilibrium angle. Variable $sigma$ (Marangoni) is out of v1 scope.

= Particles: narrow band, split, merge, and remap
== Narrow band with an Eulerian interior
The narrow band (Ferstl et al. [17]) is mandatory to meet the memory budget. Liquid particles exist only in a band of thickness $e_b>=$ support + maximum CFL displacement between reseedings + $2Delta x$, i.e. $6Delta x$ by default. *The interior is Eulerian*: `phi_grid` and $u_f$ are advected semi-Lagrangian (BFECC as a builder option) in particle-free cells. On a band face with $m_a<m_min$ but liquid according to `phi_grid`, use $u=u_"Eulerian"$ and $v_"old"=u$. A particle touching such a face explicitly sets $delta=0$ (counted local PIC); equality of the old and new grid fields alone does not cancel its particle residual. In a $2Delta x$ overlap, $w="clamp"((-phi_"grid"-e_b+2Delta x)/(2Delta x),0,1)$ blends $u^0=(1-w)u^0_"part"+w u_"Eulerian"$ before the old-grid snapshot. The liquid sign is $phi_"liq"=min(phi_"particles",phi_"grid")$: *grid occupancy is authoritative for the interior*, and closed air cavities are kept. Skinning only the particle band would otherwise create a hollow shell with a false inner surface (`surface_hollow_shell`, `deep_tank_no_inner_surface`). When the surface descends, reseed only fully liquid cells from current occupied volume $V$, current density $rho$ and advected grid pressure: $J="EOS"^(-1)(p)$, $V_(0p)=V/(n_"ppc" J)$ and $m_p=rho V/n_"ppc"$. Hencky uses $J=exp(-p/K)$; the selected Tait EOS uses its own inverse. In incompressible mode $J=1$. Initialise $v$ by gathering and $C$ by the APIC fit on the arrival support, separately from true $L$. Transfer constituent current volumes, masses and enthalpy using the same ownership rule, logging the Eulerian–particle exchange. Particles deeper than $e_b+Delta x$ are deleted. Split and merge happen only inside the band. Until M3, closed unsimulated air cavities receive only the capillary boundary pressure and may collapse; the M4 absolute-pressure cavity model below prevents this for resolved cavities.

== Physical inventory and component lineage
The band is a transport/quadrature choice, not the full liquid mass ledger. Simulate owns persistent occupancy, source and boundary inventories, and component ancestry. A semi-Lagrangian sign field may drift geometrically even when it is visually smooth; compare that drift with the physical inventory. Count every region once through the overlap, and derive render volume targets from the owned state and source/flux ledger rather than $sum J_p V_(0p)$ over the band. At finite sound speed, target volume follows mass/EOS compression rather than an incompressible constant-volume correction.

On a component split, partition the parent target by declared overlap/occupancy weights summing to one; on merge, add targets. Publish uncertainty, the chosen absolute small-component volume floor and cumulative lost volume/count. Apply the same ancestry policy to resolved cavity state. Deep-tank, translating-interface, descending-surface and true-cavity handoff tests precede production acceptance of the narrow band.

== Split 1 → 8
When a particle enters a finer level, with $Sigma_delta=D_"parent"-D_"child"$ required positive semidefinite (otherwise no split this step),
$ Sigma_delta=Q Lambda Q^T, quad delta_c=Q (s_1 sqrt(lambda_1), s_2 sqrt(lambda_2), s_3 sqrt(lambda_3)), quad s in {-1,+1}^3, $
$ x_c=x_p+delta_c, quad m_c=m_p\/8, quad V_(0c)=V_(0p)\/8, quad v_c=v_p+C_p delta_c, quad C_c=C_p, $
and $F$, $J$, history, concentrations, body and CPIC side are copied. Because $sum_c delta_c=0$ and $1\/8 sum_c delta_c delta_c^T=Sigma_delta$, this conserves mass, reference/current volume and linear momentum. Angular momentum and affine-energy claims depend on the row-specific remaining moment: zero residual gives exact energy preservation, while a residual confined to row $a$'s axis preserves the antisymmetric angular moment but removes the corresponding energy. For a uniform 2:1 quadratic split $delta=(sqrt(3)\/4)Delta x_"coarse"$ per axis. Split is restricted to uniform unmasked parent and child supports and refuses `kAffineOff`, THB transitions, obstacle or cut bands. On MAC the moments are diagonal, so take the componentwise minimum $Sigma_delta=min_a(D_(p a)-D_(c a))=(3/16)Delta x_"coarse"^2 I$. For the cubic-normal/quadratic-transverse kernel, row $a$'s residual is confined to coordinate $a$: angular momentum stays exact and the exact energy loss $m Delta x_"coarse"^2 sum_a C_(a a)^2/32$ is logged. Also refuse a child in another phase, CPIC side, obstacle or outside the fine level; the parent remains a valid quadrature point.

== Merge n → 1
Only Liquid and Gas particles of the same phase, substance and body, without `kAffineOff`, on uniform unmasked supports before and after, away from THB transitions, obstacle and cut bands, merge ($n<=8$); Elastic, Granular and Snow *never* merge, while their grid may still coarsen. With $M=sum m_c$, $overline(x)$, $overline(v)$ the mass-weighted means and $r_c=x_c-overline(x)$:
$ K=sum_c m_c (r_c r_c^T+D_c), quad H=sum_c m_c ((v_c-overline(v))r_c^T+C_c D_c), quad C^*=H D^(*-1)\/M, $
with $D^*$ the moment at $overline(x)$ on the destination support, $V_0=sum V_(0c)$, $J^*=sum J_c V_(0c)\/V_0$ and $"conc"^*=sum_c "conc"_c J_c V_(0c)/sum_c J_c V_(0c)$, preserving each constituent current volume. This conserves $m$, $V_0$, $sum J V_0$, momentum and angular momentum. The merge is *accepted only if $M D^*-K$ is positive semidefinite*: then $(M D^*)^(-1) prec.eq K^(-1)$ and the affine kinetic energy can only decrease, by a Cauchy–Schwarz argument on the stacked moments. The loss is counted (`merge_energy_loss`); otherwise the merge is refused. On MAC the construction is done *component by component*: $K_a$, row $a$ of $H$, $D^*_a$ and $c^*_a$, with diagonal $D_(c,a)$, and the semidefinite test is applied for each $a$. These formulas derive from the APIC moments of Jiang et al. [1]. Tests: `split_conservation`, `merge_energy_bound`, `merge_counterstream`. Constrained NNLS/MPCR coarsening is deferred.

== Level remap of Eulerian state <sec-remap>
On a level change, the Eulerian state (band interior, `phi_grid`, gas scalars, species) is remapped conservatively:
$ V_C=sum_j V_j, quad M_C=sum_j M_j " (each extensive scalar)", quad Q_F=sum_(f subset F) a_f u_f, quad u_F=Q_F\/a_F, $
with prolongation by parent value plus a minmod-limited slope such that children sum to the parent. A face with $a_F=0$ is blocked. Temperature is recomputed from enthalpy, never volume-averaged; pressure is a warm start and, at finite sound speed, physical advected state remapped by current-volume averaging. Physical parameters ($rho$, $mu$, $sigma$, Lamé moduli, friction) never change with level; history-dependent moduli are re-evaluated from the remapped state, not averaged. A coarse sign or averaged $phi$ is not a substitute for fractions: coarsening a cell with two interface components could delete a sheet or cavity, which the vetoes of @sec-blocks prevent. Faces, components, gauges and multigrid maps are rebuilt on the new generation. Adaptive promotion requires dimensionless partition and first-moment defects at most $10^(-6)$ in FP32, constant-pressure integrated relative residual at most $10^(-6)$ with a stated absolute floor, and the pressure solver's volume-scaled divergence limit. Nondimensionalise moments by cell width and test translations, rotations, negative coordinates, both kernels, transition ownership and masked fallback separately. Retained particles keep their affine fit $C$ as an approximation. For $Delta D=D_"new"-D_"old"$, log $Delta E=(m/2)sum_a c_a Delta D_a c_a^T$ and the angular change from the corresponding row-dependent antisymmetric moments. Coarsening is vetoed if the positive aggregate energy increase exceeds $epsilon_E E_"block"$ (initial design value 1 %, to measure). Flagged particles have zero affine contribution. Angular momentum across retained-support remap is measured rather than asserted exact.

*Thin sheets* (decided: below the finest cell they are a meshing matter, not a simulation one). Thickness measured between opposite interfaces enters $h_"interface"$; thinning regions are reseeded (Ando 2012). Below $Delta x_min$ the sheet is kept visually by the tracked mesh, without projection, following extrapolated grid velocity.

= Gas, fire, and explosions <sec-gas>
== Reduced low-Mach model
The selected *reduced physical low-Mach* model uses few artist parameters: fixed-composition air ($gamma=1.4$), $p_0$ uniform per pressure component, combustion releasing heat, oxygen not represented. Velocity is carried by APIC particles on the MAC grid; Impulse PIC [11] is a declared transfer variant requiring an ADR, because it transports impulse with its own stretching term. Particles are seeded only in active blocks. The projection is the liquid one without free surface. Gas receives *no full gravity in ⑤*: pressure is measured relative to the ambient hydrostatic state, and buoyancy is the only vertical forcing. Grid state is soot and fuel densities $c_"soot"$, $c_"fuel"$ (kg/m³), temperature $T$, and $p_0$ per component (the reduced thermodynamic model):
$ partial_t c+nabla dot (c u)=R_c, quad rho c_p D T\/D t=dot(q)+dot(p)_0+nabla dot (k nabla T), quad rho_"gas"=p_0\/(R_s T), $
with $R_s=287$ J/(kg·K) the specific gas constant, evaluated before ⑧.
Fuel, soot and enthalpy use an independently checked conservative flux discretisation with positivity treatment. A semi-Lagrangian or BFECC reconstruction by itself is not an exact mass/enthalpy balance. Recover temperature from the conserved thermodynamic state; limiter smoothness does not replace inventory checks. Reaction: $omega=min(k_r c_"fuel",c_"fuel"\/Delta t)$ if $T>T_"ign"$, $dot(q)=H_c omega$, $R_"fuel"=-omega$, $R_"soot"=+y_s omega$. The divergence source and the closed-domain pressure law are
$ s=((gamma-1)(dot(q)+nabla dot (k nabla T)))\/(gamma p_0)-dot(p)_0\/(gamma p_0), $
$ dot(p)_0=((gamma-1)(integral dot(q) dif V+integral.cont k nabla T dot n dif A)-gamma p_0 dif V_"fluid"\/dif t)\/V_"fluid" quad (0 "if open"), $
where the wall conduction term vanishes for the default adiabatic walls.
The source includes conduction. $dot(q)$ (W/m³) comes from reaction rates and sources, *never* from an Eulerian difference of $T$: a translated non-uniform temperature field must not create spurious expansion (`gas_translation_no_spurious_div`). In a closed box $dot(p)_0$ restores compatibility, and `sealed_gas_heating` checks $dif p_0\/dif t=(gamma-1)Q\/V$ within 1 %. Buoyancy is
$ b=(-a_s c_"soot"+b_T (T-T_"amb")) hat(e)_"up", quad b_T=|g|\/T_"amb", quad a_s=|g|\/rho_"amb" " (defaults)", $
so hot gas rises and soot sinks (`buoyancy_sign`). An explosion deposits energy $E$ as $dot(q)=E\/(V_"dep" t_"dep")$; in an open domain without shock the added volume is $Delta V=(gamma-1)E\/(gamma p_0)$, checked within ±25 % (`explosion_added_volume`).

Gas particles are transport markers. Their positive deposition weights are numerical quadrature with explicit initialisation/remapping, not an additional air/fuel/soot inventory. Grid thermodynamics and EOS supply physical density and pressure/viscous inertia. F2 can conserve marker deposition weight but does not thereby prove physical gas mass conservation; scalar flux and enthalpy budgets remain independent.

== Supersonic extension (reserved, not implemented) <sec-supersonic>
Shocks need conservative mass, momentum and energy with shock-stable fluxes; a divergence source is not enough. The retained path is the semi-implicit all-Mach scheme of Kwatra et al. [26], which reuses the multigrid: explicit conservative, limited advection of $(rho,rho u,E)$ with the same advector as smoke, then
$ p\/(rho c^2 Delta t^2)-nabla dot ((1\/rho) nabla p)=p^star\/(rho c^2 Delta t^2)-(nabla dot u^star)\/Delta t, quad c^2=gamma p\/rho, $
assembled through the reserved Helmholtz pair of @sec-pfv ($V_i p_i\/(rho_i c_i^2 Delta t)$ on the left, $V_i p^star_i\/(rho_i c_i^2 Delta t)$ on the right), and finally correction of $u$, $rho u$, $E$ with the same gradient. The extension requires that Helmholtz capability, lazily allocated channels $(rho,rho u,E)$ in the compressible branch, an equation-of-state interface $p=(gamma-1)rho e$, the limited flux-form advector, a pluggable time-step policy ($|u|$ or $|u|+c$), and named oracles (Sod shock tube with plateau within 10 %, Sedov blast $R(t) prop (E t^2\/rho)^(1\/5)$, and bitwise equality with the incompressible projection when $c -> infinity$). Shock–fracture coupling in the manner of Cao et al. 2022 remains a later direction.

== Weak compressibility of liquids (option, M4) <sec-weak>
The substance parameter $c_"ref"$ defines the Hencky bulk modulus $K=rho_0 c_"ref"^2$. In the reserved pressure compliance use that same tangent modulus: $rho c_"eff"^2=K$, so $c_"eff"^2=K/rho$. Tait supplies its state-dependent $K_"eff"=rho partial p/partial rho$ and matching volume-ratio inverse. Reference and current density must not silently define different compliance coefficients.

A per-substance option (M4) makes a liquid slightly compressible through the Helmholtz term. With $K=rho_0 c_"ref"^2$, the pressure history is an advected grid field $tilde(p)^n$, over both band and interior; a newly liquid cell starts at zero. Set $p^star=tilde(p)^n$ and solve
$ (V_i/(K_i Delta t))p_i+sum_f k_f(p_i-p_j)=(V_i/(K_i Delta t))p^star_i-sum_f a_f u^star_f n_(i f)-"wall flux"+dots. $
The accepted pressure becomes the next physical history and warm start. Particle $J$ evolves by $J <- J exp(Delta t tr L)$ and supports density and EOS-consistency diagnostics; recounting particles never defines pressure. The EOS choice is Hencky (default) or Tait: Hencky has $p=-K_0 ln J$, $J_"EOS"(p)=exp(-p/K_0)$, $K_0=rho_0 c_"ref"^2$; Tait has $p=B(J^(-n_T)-1)$, $B=K_0/n_T$, $J_"EOS"(p)=(1+p/B)^(-1/n_T)$, $n_T=7$ and $K_"eff"=n_T(p+B)$. Enforce $p+B>0$, including the vapour threshold, and freeze the tangent at the predictor for one declared Picard iteration with measured linearisation error. Reseeding uses the selected EOS inverse and current occupied volume as defined in the narrow-band section.

At $c=infinity$, the compliance vanishes and the incompressible density controller is unchanged. At finite $c$, use $s_"corr"="clamp"(-(ln overline(J)+tilde(p)^n/K)/t_"corr",plus.minus s_max)$ for Hencky, or generally $s_"corr"="clamp"(-(ln overline(J)-ln J_"EOS"(p^star))/t_"corr",plus.minus s_max)$, zero in the particle-free interior. This controls drift from the advected physical pressure rather than physical compression. Validate $nabla dot u-s=-(p-p^star)/(K Delta t)$, with the same residual convention as the pressure solve. Nonzero compliance retains physical mean pressure; it requires no Neumann gauge or compatibility subtraction. Positive compliance preserves SPD; any change in PCG iterations is measured, not guaranteed.

Backward Euler damps a resolved acoustic mode by $1/sqrt(1+(omega Delta t)^2)$ per step in the linear model. A midpoint option applies Crank–Nicolson to the coupled pressure/velocity system; it conserves linearized acoustic energy for fixed geometry, adjoint spatial operators and homogeneous closed boundaries, not arbitrary nonlinear Tait flow or moving geometry. Implicit acoustic stability does not guarantee resolution: use $c Delta t/Delta x lt.tilde 2$ and at least ten cells per wavelength for visible waves. The default guard $c>=max(10u_max,sqrt(100 g H))$ targets volume changes below 1 %. Smaller $c$ deliberately makes compression visible and requires checking pressure fronts, water hammer, standing-wave periods and hydrostatic compression.

Compliance is assigned only to the simulated fluid fraction; an unsimulated air cavity is counted once, by its boundary pressure. For resolved closed cavities ($R_"eq">=2Delta x$), M4 stores absolute pressure $p_(b,"abs")=p_(b,"abs",0)(V_0/V)^gamma$, and supplies gauge boundary pressure $p_Gamma=p_(b,"abs")-p_"atm"+sigma kappa$. At closure initialise to $p_"atm"$; an initially submerged spherical cavity starts at $p_"atm"+rho|g|d+2sigma/R_"eq"$. The explicit cavity restriction is $Delta t<=0.5/omega_"cav"$, $omega_"cav"=sqrt(3gamma p_(b,"abs")/rho)/R_"eq"$. Maintain cavity volume and identity lineage across splits and merges; sub-resolution disappearance is logged. Cavitation uses an active-set Dirichlet vapour-pressure clamp, with an explicit gauge convention. Acoustic energy $sum V p^2/(2K)$, density and EOS-relative drift are logged. Realistic sound remains an acoustic post-process. This MAC/APIC/free-surface synthesis and all acoustic targets require implementation and independent validation.

== Flow-map transport for gas (under a decision gate) <sec-flowmap>
A flow map tracks the map from initial to current position and its Jacobian, and transports the *impulse* $m_c=T_(a c)^T m_a$ with $T=F^(-1)$ over $n$ steps without re-interpolation, which respects Kelvin circulation and preserves vorticity much better [29]. Its $F$ is exactly the MPM $F$ and evolves the same way, but there is no constitutive law or force from it. *IPIC is PFM with $n=1$.* This is a transfer variant, not a new solver: the MAC grid, projection, fixed-point P2G and transactions are unchanged, and a pluggable `Transport` stage offers `APIC` (default), `Impulse(1,1)` (IPIC) and `Impulse(n_L>1, n_S)` (PFM). In G2P, $T_(b c) <- T_(b c)(I-Delta t L_p)$ with the same $nabla N$, $m_c=T_(a c)^T m_a$, $nabla m_c=T_(b c)^T nabla m_b T_(b c)$, and path-integrated forces $Lambda += Delta t T_(a c)^T (f_"buoy"-1\/2 nabla |u|^2)$; P2G deposits $N' m_p ((m_c-Lambda)_a+nabla m_c dot r')$. Maps reinitialise every $n_L$ (long) and $n_S$ (short) steps, and are forced to $n=1$ locally on merge, reseeding, level change or THB junction, within $2Delta x$ of bodies, cuts or contact, when $"cond"(T)>kappa_T$, or where `kAffineOff` is set. Splits copy $T$, $m_a$, $nabla m$ and $Lambda$. In the Impulse branch $alpha=beta=0$; $n_L$, $n_S$ are internal; no grid velocity-history buffer is kept.

Liquid stays APIC/ASFLIP: the cited sources do not validate flow maps combined with a two-phase ghost-fluid free surface, surface tension and a narrow band. Compressible flow keeps the flux-form advector. The low-Mach delivery baseline is APIC. Optional IPIC research must beat that baseline measurably (vortex-ring energy at 2 s at least 5 points above APIC), otherwise the branch is removed. A later PFM experiment uses its own adoption gate: at least five leapfrog passes against at most three, ring energy ≥ 90 % at 2 s, cost ≤ 1.5 × IPIC and at most +100 allocated B per gas slot (97 B raw payload; the separately aligned map-age array costs 4 B per slot rather than one); *determinism*, `determinism_bytes` passes with reinitialisation, with CPU/CUDA parity on integral metrics only. A transport interface may preserve this extension path, but no unused flow-map arrays are allocated in the APIC baseline. Nothing here is implemented or measured.

= Surface: tracked mesh and multiresolution <sec-surface>
#figure(align(center,mesh-figure()),caption:[Stage responsibilities. Simulate owns its interface geometry. Mesh is a pure consumer; Multires is an optional consumer of Mesh.])

== Responsibilities and inputs
Simulate owns `sdf_sim`, θ and κ and never reads Mesh. Mesh is a pure consumer: rerunning it with other settings changes no byte of `sim/` (`mesh_rerun_sim_hash`). Mesh runs on the *CPU*, one frame behind Simulate and in parallel with it; a GPU port (Chentanez et al. 2016) is triggered only if the budget gate of @sec-stages fails. Simulate exports per frame: `sdf_sim` (band at $Delta x$, interior sign from authoritative Eulerian occupancy), band particles (positions, radii), velocity extrapolated three cells into air with a validity mask, obstacle SDF and velocity, and per-component labels and liquid volumes (FP64).

== Motion across an output interval
Mesh and Whitewater consume a declared `MotionInterval`, not merely the velocity at the final frame. Start with timestamped carrier samples and a declared interpolation on fixed caches. Bound transport error on translated and folding surfaces; increase sampling only when that benchmark requires it, or compare a compact transport map if storage dominates. Exporting every substep without a measured storage budget is not the default. Reconstructing a tracked arbitrary frame requires a prior MeshState/checkpoint and deterministic replay.

== Target field and tracked mesh
*Candidate geometric target* `sdf_render` (OpenVDB on CPU, at $Delta x$): a Zhu–Bridson field on band particles, united by min with `sdf_sim` below the band, so there is no false bottom surface and closed air cavities survive. Compare isotropic and anisotropic kernels and constrained skinning before selecting production smoothing. The current candidate variant of Bhattacharya et al. [13] applies $K$ Laplacian iterations in the band with $phi$ bounded between $phi_"int"$ and $phi_"ext"$ from particle radii, then redistancing. There is *no temporal filter* on the SDF (it caused ghosts and fps dependence); temporal coherence comes from the mesh.

*Tracking.* Vertices advect by RK2 using `MotionInterval`, with spatial sub-steps of at most $0.5Delta x$ and separately checked temporal error. Bounded projection
$ x <- x-lambda phi nabla phi\/|nabla phi|^2, quad lambda=1-exp(-Delta t\/tau), $
with $tau$ in seconds (so independent of fps) and displacement capped at $min(0.5Delta x,0.5Delta x Delta t/tau)$ (a physical correction-rate cap), is refused if $|nabla phi|<0.5$, if it crosses another component, or if the vertex lies in an *unresolved sheet*: a ray cast along $-n$ finds the mesh again within $2Delta x_"sdf"$. Bound root search or trust-region projection by feature separation and phase identity, with obstacle exclusion, so opposite sheet sides cannot converge onto one root. Such vertices only advect; `thickness` is measured by that ray, and the sheet ruptures only below $t_"rupture"$, the hole being a local repair. Tangential relaxation then local split, collapse and flip target an edge length of $Delta x$, finer where curvature demands; no operation creates a non-manifold edge, and edge quality is restored by remeshing; physical surface stretching under advection is allowed.

*Topology changes* follow Wojtan et al. [27, 28]: the mesh's own signed occupancy is sampled on a $Delta x$ grid; a cell is *complex* if one of its edges is crossed more than once or the mesh self-intersects there. Repair happens only on a *real intersection* (merge) or a rupture decided above: the complex cells, dilated by one and closed to compatible boundaries, are cleared, rebuilt by marching cubes on exact triangle distance on a local grid $Delta x_"repair"=Delta x/2^k$, and stitched. Choose the smallest $k<=4$ with $2Delta x_"repair"<=t_min$ in the region. If no such grid resolves the sheet, rupture locally with W-MESH-SHEET-LOSS and log lost area. The output invariant is a closed, oriented, manifold, self-intersection-free surface; after three failures the component is re-extracted from `sdf_render` with W-MESH-REPAIR-FALLBACK and logged lost sheet area. Every repair is logged (region, dead and born IDs).

*Attributes.* Survivors keep `id`; new IDs come from a counter in sorted operation order (deterministic). `age` is zero for new vertices, `wetness` is interpolated, `thickness` recomputed; `velocity` is geometric displacement over $Delta t$ (motion blur) and `fluid_velocity` is sampled from the field. Stable IDs measure identity, not quality: FB3 asks for ≥ 90 % surviving IDs outside logged repair regions for a drop into a pool, ≥ 95 % in calm phases.

== Volume lock, droplets, preview
Per connected component, correct the delivered vertices rather than the target field:
$ delta="clamp"((V_"sim"-V_"mesh")/A_"mobile",plus.minus 0.25Delta x), quad x_v <- x_v+delta n_v. $
$A_"mobile"$ includes only vertices outside obstacle contact and unresolved sheets. Remeasure volume on the final mesh and check global self-intersection after each correction; halve $delta$ if it introduces an intersection. Allow at most three iterations, then W-MESH-VOLUME-INFEASIBLE; IDs remain unchanged. Raw simulation and corrected mesh volumes are published separately, with no compensation between components. A component smaller than $2Delta x$ becomes a liquid droplet (`droplets`, distinct from spray) counted in volume, returning to mesh above $3Delta x$; it has exactly one representation. Preview extraction uses `sdf_render` without tracking.

== Shared phase interfaces
Immiscible liquids require one labeled shared phase-pair interface. Independent reconstructions of each phase must not create overlaps or gaps. Triple junctions need a tested multi-material method; one plane per simulation cell does not certify them. Introduce alternative tracking or representation only when a measured shared-interface failure justifies it.

== Cage and native Multires (conditional M3 delivery)
When requested by the reference benchmark, validated cage/native Multires delivery belongs to M3. A candidate quad cage is built by manifold dual contouring on `sdf_render` at 4–8 $Delta x$, per component. A *certificate* requires the same number of components and Euler characteristic as the tracked mesh, Hausdorff distance at most $0.5Delta x$ after projection, no flipped face, and global triangle–triangle intersection checks on the projected cage and finest rendered subdivision. Every intermediate delivered LOD must also pass its own check or a conservative geometric bound; a failing component is delivered from the tracked mesh, and the full tracked mesh is always delivered. Delivery is either a Geometry Nodes group (subdivision then two bounded projection steps using triquadratic grid sampling) or a true Blender Multires: the solver delivers an already valid cage, Blender subdivides, a copy is projected, and `multires_reshape` transfers it. Existing Multires detail requires unchanged receiving topology and correspondence, since Reshape requires identical topology and indices. A topology event requires explicit detail transfer/rebuild or component fallback. Persist cage generation and interval identity, connectivity, hierarchy/level mapping and detail correspondence, checkpointing state needed for replay. A stateless per-frame cage rebuild does not establish preserved native detail. A pinned headless reader/export spike must demonstrate the intended animated or snapshot delivery without a per-render-frame Python handler before native mode is claimed. Subdivision cannot create a missing handle or droplet; the certificate is what excludes such cases. The accepted trade-off: topology changes only at real intersections; sheets thinner than $Delta x$ are kept for rendering, their physics resolved down to $Delta x_min$ and then carried by the mesh alone (decided).

#figure(align(center,meshing-dag-figure()),caption:[The simulation cache has independent consumers. A neural field backend is an experiment inside Mesh; GPU work competes with the simulator and changes the measured pipeline cost.])

== A controlled reconstruction experiment <sec-neural>
The render reconstruction is a consumer of saved simulation data. Its first experiment compares one geometric reference with the published Zhao–Shinar–Schroeder CNN [14], using identical saved particle frames. Evaluate the CNN twice: once with the complete saved particle set and once with the actual intended reconstruction band. Keep network architecture, weights, pruning rules, scalar-grid spacing and query locations fixed. This separates a missing-input error from the model's own reconstruction error. A favourable result may justify a later cache or backend decision; it does not automatically change the simulation band or select a neural production dependency.

The source-inspected artifact is the 2025 PACMCGIT paper, Article 51, DOI 10.1145/3747856. The inspected PDF SHA-256 is
#block(inset:5pt,fill:gray)[#text(font:"DejaVu Sans Mono",size:7.3pt)[d314b6455cea5b680dcdf78737d5a021e2657e029ce1b5aac8f935e0355a81a2]]
and the published source ZIP SHA-256 is
#block(inset:5pt,fill:gray)[#text(font:"DejaVu Sans Mono",size:7.3pt)[bc4304606c6b378bfd06dec928025e38d8cf6fd767f70a43d8bdc0f56c3e16ca]]
The source archive includes a pretrained model and ten dam-break frames. It was inspected, not built or executed. No third-party archive, source tree or pretrained weights are stored in this repository. The article's CC BY 4.0 statement does not establish software or weight rights: the ZIP lacks a standalone license, references separately obtained PhysBAM, and supplies no identified separate weight license. Distribution or adoption requires resolving those terms.

The shipped network receives an $8^3$ scalar patch: four unpadded $2^3$ convolutions, with channels $1,64,128,256,512$, a size-two pool after the second convolution, then a $512,256,128,1$ multilayer perceptron. There are 1,542,081 parameters including biases. The supplied ONNX shape is fixed at $[1024,1,8,8,8]$ with output $[1024,1]$; the native CUDA batching and final padding also assume 1024. Reusing the published path therefore requires coordinated batching changes for other batch sizes, rather than assuming arbitrary dynamic input.

=== Source-derived support of the shipped configuration
Let $h_phi$ denote the CLI output-grid spacing and $h_N="stride" h_phi$ the spacing of an inference subgrid. In the shipped stride-two evaluation, $h_phi=h_N/2$ and the quartic kernel radius is $2.5h_N$. For one evaluated scalar-grid query $q$, the eight feature positions along each axis are $q+(j-3.5)h_N$, $j=0,...,7$. A feature depends on contributing particles, whose weights divide by an interpolated particle-density estimate. That estimate in turn depends on more particles. Consequently the dependency chain is
$ "query" -> "feature sample" -> "particle" -> "density node" -> "density particle", $
and a conservative axis-aligned neighbourhood is
$ R_("dep",infinity)<=3.5h_N+2.5h_N+h_phi+2.5h_N=9h_N. $
Here $h_"sim"$ is independent of $h_N$ and $h_phi$. This is a source-derived $L^infinity$ enclosure around each evaluated scalar query for that configuration. It is neither a minimum support nor a Euclidean radius; an enclosing Euclidean ball may need $9sqrt(3)h_N$. The source's accumulation stencil is specialised to stride two, so a different stride, kernel, feature or interpolation requires a new derivation. Query-selection uncertainty, complete extraction cells and postprocessing need additional halos. A particle band measured by surface distance cannot be identified directly with this query-centred box.

Keeping only particles that directly touch the feature patch omits the density denominator's dependence. Replacing missing bulk by caps, coarse particles or hand-filled density changes the input distribution until a full-cache comparison establishes equivalence. The published occupied-cell pruning and skipped-node values must also match across the full/band runs; they are not a proof that bulk data are irrelevant. Measure the actual retained support, field error and surface error before proposing any cache enlargement.

#figure(align(center,neural-halo-figure()),caption:[The source-derived CNN dependency follows feature construction and its density denominator. The $9h_N$ bound is a query-centred axis-aligned enclosure for the shipped stride-two configuration, distinct from a surface-distance particle band.])

Before promotion, compare tiled and monolithic inference with complete dependency halos, a fixed global origin, matched pruning and identical batching/order. Filling known deep liquid negative in the output field can prevent an extraction shell but does not repair missing network inputs. If any coverage or fidelity gate fails, retain the geometric backend; band widening, new occupancy channels or retraining are distinct experiments whose total simulation/cache cost must be measured.

=== Cost, quality, and a bounded hybrid backend
On its reference platform, the paper reports 2.21 s for one two-million-particle frame on an RTX 4090 and a 32-core Threadripper PRO 5975WX with 512 GB RAM: 1.01 s model inference, 0.90 s input/output and model loading, and about 0.30 s CPU work. The 1.31 s figure excludes loading and I/O. The executable produces scalar arrays or VDB; its README describes later polygon conversion in Houdini. These timings therefore do not certify an end-to-end delivered polygon mesh, and they are not FluidiTy measurements. Record feature construction, inference, extraction, repairs, transfer, I/O, cold start, steady state and peak memory separately on the target machine.

Test translated smooth surfaces, sheets and gaps of thickness $2h,h,h/2,h/4$, droplets, wall contact, cavities, folding sheets and topology changes, including adaptive particle density. Report scalar and zero-set disagreement with full-data CNN separately from geometric-reference quality: distance and normal error, raw per-component volume bias, lost components, cavity/thickness retention, temporal artifacts and residual after known advection. Evaluate manifoldness and global intersections after extraction. Neither a smooth field nor a stable-looking sequence establishes tracked vertex identity.

A later hybrid research backend may use a learned target field inside the existing tracked-mesh pipeline while retaining explicit advection, bounded projection, local repair, IDs and volume correction. This changes field construction only; it does not turn a network into a collision detector, a topology proof, a physical capillary model or a substitute for missing simulation history. Compare it with the full classical pipeline at matched resolution and memory, with a deletion path if the measured benefit does not justify complexity. No automatic neural adoption follows from implementing the experiment. If promoted, persistently load one versioned model and freeze its execution artifact for the bake. Fingerprint model bytes, preprocessing, physical scales, query origin/order, batch policy, precision and inference engine; a model name alone does not fix engine tactics. Checkpoint the prior mesh, backend identity, lineage and ID counter. CPU model parity uses the same model and preprocessing; geometric fallback is a separately named backend. Bound immutable frame queues, copy/event lifetimes, backpressure and the shared CPU thread pool. Enable concurrent neural and simulator kernels only after measuring benefit on their shared GPU.

