= Whitewater
A grid variant of Ihmsen et al. [12] uses specific potentials independent of particle mass. With $Phi(I,tau_min,tau_max)="clip"((I-tau_min)/(tau_max-tau_min),0,1)$ and ordered unit-compatible thresholds,
$ I_k=|u_c|^2/2, quad I_"ta"=sum_(j!=c)(|u_c-u_j|-(u_c-u_j) dot hat(x)_(c j))W(|x_c-x_j|), $
$ I_"wc"=max(0,kappa_c)[u_c dot n_c>=0.6|u_c| " and " |u_c|>0], quad Phi_c=Phi(I_k)(k_"ta" Phi(I_"ta")+k_"wc" Phi(I_"wc")). $
Use the 26 neighbouring source cells and $W(r)=max(0,1-r/(2r_"ww"))$. Exclude self and zero-distance pairs. The potential units are m²/s², m/s and m⁻¹ respectively; the $k$ coefficients are dimensionless. Invalid normal or curvature raises an explicit diagnostic, never a NaN hidden by multiplication by zero. Normals and curvature come from `sdf_sim`; zero velocity gives no crest source without normalising it. The source grid has fixed scene spacing $r_"ww"$ in metres, independent of quality, camera and AMR; each region has one owner. A source requires valid velocity and occupied liquid volume $V_c>0$.

Step ⑫ exports one trial event $(t_s,x_c,u_c,n_c,V_c Phi_c Delta t_s)$ per nonzero source cell and accepted sub-step, without temporal averaging. Two opposite bursts within a frame remain separate. Capacity is checked (E-WW-SOURCE-CAPACITY); rejection discards its events and source state. The event weight stores occupied volume at birth, $w_s=V_c(t_s)Phi_c Delta t_s$, rather than looking it up at the final frame. Whitewater emits $n=q w_s/w_"out"$ plus a carried remainder, with $q$ in m⁻³·s⁻¹, dimensionless potentials and $w_"out"$ physical particles per output point. Each point is born at the declared quadrature time $t_s$ and integrated through `MotionInterval` to output time. Compare counts and position/velocity/age distributions under output-rate changes; fixed-frame RNG keys alone do not prove fps independence. Changing the foam amount does not rerun Simulate. Points are distributed in a cylinder of radius $r_"ww"$ with source velocity plus radial noise.

Depth $d$ in `sdf_sim` gives spray for $d>r_c$, bubbles for $d<-r_c$, foam otherwise, with hysteresis $0.5r_c$. For frozen driving fields, spray and bubbles use the exact velocity and position update
$ v^+=v_"eq"+(v-v_"eq")e^(-k_d Delta t), quad x^+=x+v_"eq"Delta t+(v-v_"eq")(1-e^(-k_d Delta t))/k_d, $
with $v_"eq"=v_"air"+g/k_d$ for spray and $v_"eq"=u_f-k_b g/k_d$ for bubbles; $k_d$ is s⁻¹ and $k_b$ dimensionless. Evaluate with `expm1` and analytic zero-drag limits. Foam follows $x^+=x+Delta t u_f$ and expires by age. Outside velocity validity, spray remains ballistic with exact ambient-wind drag, bubbles retain their class and explicitly use $u_f=0$, and foam requires its exported carrier halo: a missing field raises E-WW-MISSING-CARRIER with coordinate and time, requiring cache repair or re-export, without class change or ballistic fallback. Never interpret an invalid VDB sample as a valid zero velocity. Collisions use the obstacle geometry and continuous-motion contract.

Coupling is one-way: Whitewater reads `sim/`, never `mesh/`; its points do not displace liquid and cannot model aeration feedback. Philox emission keys use (source cell, global accepted sub-step, ordinal), and dynamics use (id, frame). Geometry Nodes projects foam from `whitewater/`; Mesh never reads it. Multi-scale bubbles remain a future direction.

= State, storage, and memory
== Particle state
Particles are AoSoA in packets of 32, one 128 B-aligned array per physical field, one stream per (block, continuum). *Persistent* fields survive the sub-step and are checkpointed; *scratch* fields are rebuilt every sub-step. A particle index changes when sorting; `id` never does.

