= Execution contracts
== GPU execution <sec-gpu>
Kernels launch over *capacity* (or grid-stride) with early exit on a device-side live count. Arena pointers pass through a device control block, so captured execution growth between frames first attempts `cudaGraphExecUpdate` and recaptures/reinstantiates if changed CUB nodes cannot update. CUB primitives are captured at capacity with neutral inactive inputs, queried scratch at capacity and a pinned CCCL version; conditional handles are initialised on every entry. The measured graph-capture optimisation, once validated, nests `while` (sub-steps) ⊃ `while` (attempts) ⊃ `if` (status), with no allocation in conditional bodies [16]. G2P2G fusion (G2P of step $n$ joined with P2G of $n+1$ [5]) is allowed only with the same topology generation, no scheduled emission or adaptation, a continuum without $Delta t$-dependent constraint, and a certified uniform support; otherwise G2P and P2G run separately, and the early deposition belongs to the trial. There is *no allocation inside a sub-step*; capacity differs from live count, and overflow is CAPACITY, never a silent loss. Precision: fixed-point i64 P2G; FP32 state; FP64 for clocks, conservation sums, PCG dot products and the coarse level; BF16 forbidden. Stress (matrix work, SVD), pressure (global sparse solve) and mesh repair (irregular topology) have different work profiles and are profiled as such [15].

== Transactions <sec-tx>
A committed state $A$ and a trial $B$. Particles are double-buffered: the trial writes $B$, $A$ is never touched, and a rejection costs no copy. Persistent Eulerian fields (`u`, `phi_liquid`, `p_prev`, gas inventories and physical source inventory) have versions $n$ / $n+1$, swapped on acceptance. Whitewater birth events are private trial append records; acceptance appends them to the accepted frame stream and rejection discards them. Artist scaling and fractional whitewater emission remainders belong only to the rerunnable Whitewater stage. Topology carries a generation $g$ and is double-buffered: accepted A remains immutable, trial B owns its keys, maps and fields. After ACCEPTED, adaptation is a separate transaction reading the accepted state and writing the free buffer; local refusal leaves the particle unchanged, capacity invokes the frame restart. A device control block (≈ 1 KB, copied into the trial) holds FP64 time, $Delta t$, next `id`, fractional physical particle/fuel source credits (FP64), RNG counter, attempt count and status. Statuses:
+ ACCEPTED: versions swap; the clock advances exactly once.
+ RETRY_DT (CFL violated a posteriori, pressure not converged, $det F<=0$, inadmissible $F$, trial NaN): restart from $A$ at $Delta t\/2$, at most twice, then an E- error.
+ CAPACITY: leave the graph, grow arenas, restart the frame from the frame-start snapshot (@sec-stages), at most once, then E-MEMORY-BUDGET.
+ FATAL (CUDA error, overflow, corruption): stop.
An incompatible pressure right-hand side is an error, not a retry. Repeatedly halving $Delta t$ never repairs a full table.

== Determinism and CPU/CUDA parity
Bitwise reproducibility on the same GPU, driver and build relies on: every mass, pure-momentum and force contribution quantised per particle before any sum, $Q="rint"(c/s_c)$ in i64 with a logged scale per channel. Before conversion require finite $c$ and check $|c/s_c|<=2^(62-n_"cap")$, where $2^n_"cap"$ bounds total particle capacity; this bounds absolute sums independently of cancellations. Failure is FATAL E-P2G-RANGE, with particle and channel, never saturation. Actual contributions include affine velocity, mass and stress; a velocity cap alone is insufficient; stable counting sort; active blocks sorted by key at every generation; floating reductions in a fixed tree (CUB `run_to_run`), with floating scans forbidden without that guarantee; IDs allocated by scan on (parent id, ordinal); Philox RNG with one key per consumer, (id, frame, accepted sub-step) for particles and (source cell, global accepted sub-step, ordinal) for whitewater emission; serialisation by increasing `id`. CPU and CUDA share the same `FLUIDITY_HD` kernel bodies and quantisation and are compared by tolerance: per kernel, P2G within one quantum per contribution and G2P $|Delta x|<=10^(-6)$ m; per sub-step, $norm(Delta p)_infinity<=10 "tol" p_"ref"$ and $norm(Delta v)_infinity<=10^(-4) v_"ref"$; per trajectory, integral metrics only (dam break at 0.5 s: front ±1 $Delta x$, volume ±0.1 %, kinetic energy ±2 %).

== Stages, manifests, and checkpoints <sec-stages>
Stages are separate and rerunnable: *Simulate* (GPU) writes `sim/` (band VDB grids `sdf_sim`, velocity and mask, obstacle SDF; band particles; components; sparse whitewater sources; stats, logs, trace; full particles at checkpoints and, as an advanced debug option, every frame); *Mesh* (CPU, frame $N-1$, in parallel) writes `mesh/` (Alembic tracked mesh with `id`, `velocity`, `fluid_velocity`, `age`, `wetness`, `thickness`, `substance`, `phase`, plus `droplets`); *Whitewater* writes `whitewater/` points; *Volumes* writes foam, bubble, liquid SDF, velocity and gas density, temperature, fuel and flame VDBs; *Multires* writes the validated cage and `sdf_render`. Rerunning a stage invalidates only its consumers; rerunning Mesh invalidates only Multires.

Each stage has a manifest (candidate FlatBuffers representation with a JSON mirror) holding status (`baking` with pid and heartbeat, `complete`, `cancelled`, `error`; a `baking` manifest whose process died reads as `crashed`), completed frames, and a content fingerprint with a documented hash algorithm over schema, the stage's own *resolved* settings in canonical serialisation, external data hashes, build and backend hashes, and upstream *content* sums (never manifest bytes). Publication is atomic (temporary file, rename, manifest last); the signal handler only writes a preformatted buffer with `write(2)`. A frame-start snapshot copies persistent state asynchronously to pinned host RAM (≈ 1.5 GB). An event/fence prevents reuse or mutation of source buffers until the copy completes. Its version includes topology, pressure history, control/source state and hysteresis; it serves CAPACITY restarts, crash dumps and checkpoints.

