Bound the immutable frame queue and apply backpressure. The end-to-end three-hour target for 240 frames permits 45 s per frame on average, including startup, pipeline fill/drain, all consumers and I/O. Stage medians alone cannot establish it. A candidate allocation is 32 s Simulate GPU, up to 2 s neural Mesh GPU and 6 s Whitewater/Volumes GPU per frame, overlapping up to 33 s CPU Mesh/Multires service with separately bounded I/O. These are allocations to test, not independent timing promises. Measure shared-GPU/CPU/storage contention, cold model load, initialization and queue drain; apply backpressure and do not assume Mesh stays off the critical path.

== Protocol, settings and Blender delivery
The subprocess emits only versioned JSON Lines on stdout and structured logs on stderr, with a heartbeat at least 1 Hz reporting the last coherently known progress. A stdin `cancel` request stops at a safe boundary while retaining published output; timeout fallback uses platform-appropriate termination. All `bpy` calls stay on Blender's main thread, using timer polling and transient job state outside persistent Blender ID data.

Materialise defaults in persisted resolved scene/settings records and fingerprints (`ForceDefaults` or equivalent), so a later default cannot reinterpret an old scene or cache. Numeric field/enum IDs are stable and CI runs `flatc --conform`; Python dictionaries do not define schema evolution. Deliver versioned `.blend` assets for points, Surface LOD, Whitewater, foam projection, attribute colours and volumes, with water/foam and whitewater materials. Headless tests use the same public inputs as artists. Native cache readers must demonstrate render playback without a Python callback per frame.

= Verification contracts and evidence boundaries
== Validation
Unit oracles must be independent of the implementation: B-spline nesting masks and Greville reproduction, `patch_test_t_junction`, `mac_apic_conservation`, `apic_c_across_transition`, `work_identity`, `flip_gravity_free_fall`, `asflip_alpha_once`, `asflip_beta_gate`, `g2p_order_sentinel`, `elastic_null_mode`, `cpic_mask_identities`, `flip_residual_wall`, split/merge bounds, two-phase and two-layer flux with θ, static-drop jump orientation, moving-paddle flux, sealed-box compatibility, cut-cell divergence, SPD checks, constitutive invariants, sign tests (density correction, buoyancy, volume offset), and infrastructure tests (layout ABI, fixed-point order invariance, sort determinism, bitwise rollback). Functional tests cover the dam break and energy look-book, hydrostatic column, volume drift, piston work, Rayleigh drop, Couette and planar extension, wave relaxation, band crossing, hollow-shell and deep-tank surfaces, gas vortex ring, explosion and sealed heating, whitewater sub-step invariance and ballistic halo, stage invalidation, checkpoint resume and CPU/CUDA parity. Additional oracles cover masked constant/affine gathering, old/new ghost no-force FLIP, compressed reseed/delete/level cycles, constituent-volume merge, generalised viscosity eigenvalues on slivers and contrasts, the thin-slab continuous sweep, full acoustic directional differentiation, retained-support defect budgets, opposing whitewater bursts and stiff exact bubble drag. Reliability acceptance additionally requires 50 crash/cancel/device-error injection runs with every published frame readable, 1,000 cumulative default-scene frames without unresolved retries, nonfinite accepted state or warnings, deterministic interrupted/resumed output, and at most 1 % unexplained live-memory growth from frame 10 to 240 in the fixed job. Separate live allocations, reserved high-water arenas and expected scene/output growth. All supported examples/defaults must succeed before release; one successful scene is insufficient. A skipped test is not a passed test. Budgets are < 30 s unit, < 5 min functional, < 30 min end-to-end.

== Independent numerical reference and formal contracts <sec-formal>
A small independent float64 NumPy/SciPy reference is an acceptance prerequisite for optimising each corresponding numerical feature. Its uniform 2D MAC model directly assembles tiny pressure and viscosity matrices and uses a direct sparse solve. It covers transfer, closed and free-surface pressure, moving-wall flux, source compatibility, weak compliance, explicit and implicit viscosity, and particle/finite-volume ownership. Independent 3D algebraic fixtures cover staggered angular momentum and cubic MAC split. Export versioned intermediate arrays with units, state versions, residual conventions and tolerances for C++ CPU and CUDA differential checks. Python does not implement Blender, production sparse allocation, a mesher or a second full simulator.

The production CPU backend tests the actual layouts, schema, I/O and computational path; agreement with CUDA alone cannot detect a shared assembly error. Python and production must therefore have independent assembly implementations. A fixture must exercise a stated hypothesis and include a counterexample that fails when that hypothesis is removed.

The initial Lean 4 scope is the following ten finite-dimensional theorem families [34], using finite index types, real vectors and matrices, integer reductions and a pure transition model. They are stage commitments, not interchangeable optional pilot suggestions. The complete obligations and numerical bridges are stated in the accompanying #link("../references/formal-contracts.md")[formal-contract reference]; this section records their scientific scope.

#figure(align(center,validation-figure()),caption:[Independent assembly, exact contracts, production parity and target-device evidence answer different questions. None stands in for the others.])

=== Transfer and resampling contracts
*F1: reconstruction.* For a complete row stencil require $sum_i w_i=1$, $sum_i w_i r_i=0$, invertible $D_a=sum_i w_i r_i r_i^T$, and derivative moments $sum_i nabla w_i=0$, $sum_i r_i(nabla w_i)^T=I$. Substitution of $u_a(x_i)=v_a+c_a r_i$ proves the gather, affine fit and derivative separately reproduce their affine values. Identical old/new arrays give zero grid increment; eliminating the particle FLIP residual additionally requires its particle value to equal the gathered old field. Ghost values must equal the same compatible affine continuation. Renormalisation of a masked stencil establishes partition, not its original first moment; the theorem does not supply missing ghost completeness.

The uniform F1 deliverable also defines the actual quadratic/cubic weights and proves their piecewise-polynomial identities; merely assuming partition and moments is not that complete target. THB remains subject to explicit hypothesis checks.

*F2: unmasked MAC deposition.* Complete unmasked first-moment stencils conserve mass and linear momentum per component. For physical mass deposition the three staggered arrays represent the same mass, not three additive masses. In the Gas marker branch the corresponding theorem concerns numerical deposition weight, with physical species inventories checked separately. For components $j,k$, the discrete angular momentum is
$ sum_("faces" k)x_j q_k-sum_("faces" j)x_k q_j=sum_p m_p[x_(p j)v_(p k)-x_(p k)v_(p j)+c_(p k)D_(p k)e_j-c_(p j)D_(p j)e_k]. $
Expand $x_i=x_p+r_i$ and use the row-specific second moments. The claim excludes force, contact, remap and quantisation; cut/ghost budgets are separate.

*F3: symmetric split.* Symmetric offsets have zero mean and covariance $S$. With $R_a=D_("parent",a)-D_("child",a)-S$, direct expansion gives child energy equal to parent energy minus $(m/2)sum_a c_a R_a c_a^T$. If $R_a$ is supported only along coordinate $a$, angular momentum remains exact. Quadratic isotropic splitting has zero deficit. Cubic-normal/quadratic-transverse MAC instead has $S=3h^2 I/16$, $R_a=h^2 e_a e_a^T/16$ and exact loss $m h^2 sum_a C_(a a)^2/32$. The theorem assumes uniform unmasked admissible supports; it does not prove that children avoid obstacles.

*F4: merge.* Positive masses and positive-definite row moments define $K_a,H_a$ and the target affine row as in the merge equations. The gate $M D^*_a-K_a>=0$ for every row bounds affine energy by the child energy through the block-Gram inequality and inverse order. First and antisymmetric moments prove linear and angular momentum; additive reference/current volume and current-volume-weighted concentrations preserve constituent volumes. A floating eigenvalue tolerance must refuse uncertain gates rather than treating exact PSD as a numerical guarantee.

=== Pressure and dissipation contracts
*F5: pressure quadratic form and kernel.* For integrated incidence $R$, positive face conductance $k$, and nonnegative Dirichlet/compliance diagonals $d,c$,
$ A=R "diag"(k)R^T+"diag"(d+c), quad p^T A p=sum_f k_f(R^T p)_f^2+sum_i(d_i+c_i)p_i^2. $
Every term is nonnegative. The kernel consists of constants on each unanchored positive-conductance component, vanishing on anchored components. This gives SPD after the correct gauges and solvability exactly when the right-hand side sums to zero on each unanchored component. A positivity lemma alone does not prove the kernel or compatibility conclusions.

*F6: correction residual.* With consistent incidence, jumps, wall flux and boundary lifting, take $b=S-R F^star-W+C p_"old"$, $A=L+C$ and $R F_"new"=R F^star+L p$. For $r=b-A p$, substitution gives
$ R F_"new"+W-S+C(p-p_"old")=-r. $
The identical coefficients must appear in assembly and correction. Positive cell-volume division yields the physical stopping criterion; finite compliance includes its volume-change term, so nonzero divergence alone is not a failed solve.

*F7: diffusion energy.* With positive diagonal $M$, $K=E^T W E>=0$ and a certified $K M^(-1)K prec.eq Lambda K$, explicit Euler is nonexpansive in $E_M(u)=u^T M u/2$ when $Delta t Lambda<=2$. Expansion gives the energy identity in @sec-viscous-bound; the selected Gershgorin-to-spectral bridge is itself a proof obligation. Backward Euler with wall forcing satisfies
$ E_M(u^+)-E_M(u)=Delta t (u^+)^T b_"wall"-Delta t(u^+)^T K u^+-(1/2)norm(u^+-u)_M^2. $
This proves dissipation relative to wall work; it makes no absolute contraction claim for a moving wall.

=== Execution contracts and acceptance gates
*F8: integer deposition.* For $N<=2^n$, $0<=n<=62$, $s>0$, finite $c_j$ and nearest-rounded $Q_j="rint"(c_j/s)$ with $|c_j/s|<=2^(62-n)$, every partial sum has absolute value at most $sum|Q_j|<=2^62$. Integer commutativity gives order invariance, and $|s sum Q_j-sum c_j|<=N s/2$. State ties-to-even explicitly. The theorem does not establish identical floating contributions on CPU and GPU, or safe conversion of NaN.

*F9: transactions.* A pure state transition preserves immutable committed state on rejection; induction over rejected attempts preserves particles, grid, topology, pressure history, physical source inventory, accepted events, IDs/RNG and accepted time. One acceptance swaps once and adds its accepted $Delta t$ once. Attempt diagnostics and the smaller retry step are separate outputs. Adaptation is separately transactional. Deterministic replay assumes a deterministic trial function; it does not prove CUDA race freedom or C++ nonaliasing.

*F10: tile inclusion.* The half-offset bin implies $X_0 in [4B+1.5,4B+5.5)$; with $|X-X_0|<=0.5$, $X in [4B+1,4B+6)$. Floor inequalities place the three quadratic samples for shifts zero and one-half, and the four cubic-normal samples, in $[4B,4B+8)$. Tensor products give the uniform $8^d$ tile. This assumes the displacement bound; it proves neither its estimator nor THB support inclusion. Runtime inclusion checks remain mandatory.

